{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_15","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,160p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt70/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Domain truth and settlement\n\n## Gate\n\nFreeze business facts before capability discovery. Platform capabilities decide how accepted facts map to Runtime; they do not decide what the facts are.\n\n| Kind | Meaning | Implementation consequence |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Occurred fact | A completed business event and its event-time inputs, rules, and result | Persist when it occurs; correct by reversal or adjustment |\n| Summary | A read-only aggregation of occurred facts | Use Dataset or native SQL; never own the formula that created the fact |\n| Simulation | A hypothetical result under assumed inputs or rules | Isolate from official facts, ledgers, payroll, and reports |\n\nFor every material calculation, decide: Calculation Owner, Evaluation Time, Persisted Result, Policy/Rule Snapshot, Input Snapshot, Calculation Trace, Correction/Reversal Strategy, Downstream Consumers, and Report Source of Truth. Keep only the smallest affected modeling slice blocked when accepted evidence leaves at least two incompatible choices that change the explicitly required formula, amount/unit semantics, occurred-fact identity, correction behavior, or observable result. Exact decimal representation, a stated threshold, and summation remain modelable when optional currency, unit, or range metadata is absent; use nullable metadata and stated command preconditions, and do not invent conversion rules. Route Runtime-owned representations through contract discovery and reversible persistence, index, audit, idempotency, and concurrency mechanics through the `safe_implementation_decision` rule in [development-guide.md](development-guide.md#requirements-to-domain-facts); those choices do not become product business ambiguity, but their later verification remains mandatory.\n\n## Versioned settlement pattern\n\n```text\nVersioned Policy\n  → Atomic Settlement Action\n  → Calculation Snapshot\n  → Immutable Ledger\n  → Reversal / Adjustment\n  → Native SQL Report\n  → Server-paginated Consumers\n  → Same-Report bounded threshold router (sync exhausted <=1000 / async on row 1001)\n  → Async Terminal Notification\n  → Payroll / Export Reconciliation\n```\n\nUse this pattern for an event that locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input. In one transaction, the settlement Action must validate idempotency and authorization, resolve the event-time policy, and persist policy identity/version, the actual rule snapshot, input snapshot, ordered calculation trace, final result, immutable ledger entries, and audit/outbox effects. A later policy version affects only later events.\n\nNever update or delete an original financial ledger entry to correct history. Append a linked reversal for cancellation/refund and a reasoned adjustment for an approved correction. Preserve `reversal_of` or equivalent lineage, the original ledger ID, actor, time, reason, and approval boundary. A locked payroll/export consumer records the ledger IDs or a governed scope/hash it consumed; later adjustments follow the explicit correction period instead of silently changing the locked result.\n\n## Anti-patterns\n\n- Repeating one formula across pages or several reports.\n- Joining current Settings to recompute a historical financial result.\n- Rendering absent or unproven facts as numeric zero.\n- Giving summary, detail, page, payroll, and export different scopes or formulas.\n- Allowing Settings changes to drift historical results.\n- Omitting ledger-ID-level traceability across consumers.\n- Building a project-specific report AST, metric DSL, or SQL generator.\n- Returning an unbounded list or fetching all rows for browser-memory pagination.\n- Bypassing the fixed 1000-row router, using a second export-only query, or producing duplicate artifacts for an equivalent canonical fingerprint.\n- Completing or failing an export without a notification linked to its audit, job, and artifact, or allowing an unaudited/unexpired-but-out-of-scope download.\n\n## Acceptance matrix\n\nCover applicable boundaries with event-time input, policy identity, snapshot/ledger IDs, trace, consumer result, and allow/deny evidence:\n\n- policy version switch before/after effective time;\n- business-day cutoff and cross-midnight events;\n- multi-party allocation with deterministic remainder handling;\n- full/partial refund and reversal lineage;\n- correction after payroll or another consumer is locked;\n- summary/detail/page/payroll/export reconciliation by ledger ID;\n- cross-store and cross-entity permission/data-scope denial;\n- Runtime restart and Settings change without historical drift.\n- maximum-page enforcement and stable cursor traversal without duplicate or missing rows, including equal sort keys;\n- explicit `truncated` and exact/estimated/omitted `total` semantics;\n- exact permission-filtered totals at 0, 1000, and 1001 with synchronous/asynchronous routing and identical Report/parameters/source/Scope;\n- concurrent, running, completed, expired, failed/cancelled, source-change and Scope-change fingerprint replay semantics;\n- asynchronous completed and failed terminal notifications linked to export audit/job/artifact, plus requester/Scope/expiry/integrity/audit download denial.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_16","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt70/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/backend-model.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Backend model files\n\n`backend/model/*.json` is the backend metadata inventory and source of truth; `backend/reports/*.sql` is the source-owned SQL surface referenced by Report model items. The Blueprint Ledger under `domain/` and everything under `.domainry/` are CLI-owned projections that `model apply` converges from those sources. Never edit `domain/**`, rendered Blueprints, or `.domainry/**` by hand, and never treat them as an authoring input.\n\n## File contract\n\nEvery file under `backend/model/` matching `*.json` is loaded, in lexical filename order:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"contract_version\": \"domainry-backend-model-v1\",\n  \"items\": [\n    {\n      \"module\": \"project\",\n      \"kind\": \"project\",\n      \"key\": \"project\",\n      \"payload\": {\"blueprint\": {\"schema_version\": \"1\", \"id\": \"gym\", \"name\": \"Gym\", \"description\": \"Gym operations\"}}\n    },\n    {\n      \"module\": \"membership\",\n      \"kind\": \"actor_role\",\n      \"key\": \"membership-roles\",\n      \"requirement_keys\": [\"membership-management\"],\n      \"depends_on\": [\"project/project/project\"],\n      \"payload\": {\"emits\": []}\n    }\n  ]\n}\n```\n\nItem fields:\n\n- `module`, `kind`, `key` — required identity. `kind` is one of `project`, `requirement`, `actor_role`, `object_relation`, `action_authorization`, `workflow_connector`, `acceptance`, `resource`. Identity segments are stable lowercase keys; the unique project item is always `project/project/project`.\n- `status` — optional, `closed` (default) or `decision_required`; `decision_required` must match at least one unresolved decision.\n- `requirement_keys`, `depends_on`, `decisions` — optional arrays (default empty). `depends_on` entries are full `module/kind/key` addresses.\n- `payload` — the item body (default `{}`), identical to the Ledger item payload contract for that kind.\n\nOne `module/kind/key` may be declared exactly once across all model files. Group items by owning module — typically one file per module (`backend/model/<module>.json`). Do not add envelope fields (`contract_version`, `module`, `kind`, `key`) inside `payload`, and do not nest `status`, `requirement_keys`, `depends_on`, or `decisions` there.\n\n## Requirement payload\n\nA `requirement` item's `payload.requirement` carries the requirement statement plus its `behaviors[]` array of behavior-owner tuples (owner, resource_key, and the tuple fields defined in [behavior owner decision](capabilities/behavior-owner-decision.md)). An empty `behaviors` array is legal only when the payload also supplies a non-empty `no_executable_behavior_reason` string explaining why the requirement owns no executable behavior (for example, purely declarative authorization/navigation policy carried by roles, data permissions, and views). The field must be absent when `behaviors` is non-empty; `model validate` fails closed on both violations (`empty behaviors require a reason` / `ledger.requirement_incomplete`).\n\nFor `object_sql_v1`, use exactly one project-owned SQL file named after the Report key instead of embedding a large escaped SQL string. Replace only the `sql` member with `\"sql_file\":\"backend/reports/<report-key>.sql\"`. `model validate|plan|apply` requires that exact canonical project-relative regular file, rejects symlinks/traversal and simultaneous `sql` + `sql_file`, normalizes line endings and a final newline, records its SHA-256 in the apply receipt, and embeds the normalized SQL into the existing published Runtime contract. `sql_file` is not a Runtime field or a query DSL; Runtime still receives `object_sql_v1.sql`, and `model export` may therefore emit the compatible inline form.\n\n## Commands\n\n```text\n\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" model preflight        --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\"\n\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" model validate --json --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\"\n\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" model plan     --json --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\"\n\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" model apply    --json --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> [--environment <name> | --service <url>] [--allow-destructive] [--sync-only] [--evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>]\n\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" model export   --json --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\" [--force]\n```\n\n- `preflight` is the authoritative no-HTTP, local-read-only repair loop. It reuses the same model/SQL loader, strict item contract, Ledger graph, in-process authoring checks, deterministic Project Contract projector, and Runtime-manifest compiler as the formal path. It atomically replaces `.domainry/builder/disposable/model-preflight-result.json` for both `valid` and `repair_required`; stdout contains only `state`, `issue_count`, the project-relative artifact locator, SHA-256, and identity. A repair result exits non-zero. Read the artifact from the first Gate run and batch-repair it; do not rerun a failed Gate merely to obtain diagnostics and do not implement a bespoke `jq`/regex preflight. This local boundary does not claim live Plane, Runtime configuration, provider, database, or scenario checks.\n- `validate` parses every model file, enforces the item contract, and reports the pending plan counts without writing model/Ledger state.\n- `plan` additionally lists the exact item IDs classified as create, update, or delete.\n- `apply` is the single release command: it converges the Ledger to the model files, gates on the aggregated metadata checklist, then chains live validation, signed publication, dependency resolution, backend materialization/evolution, and source finalization, stopping at the first failing stage. Every release apply resolves the dependency seed in staging before it installs files or writes a successful materialization receipt; a resolution failure stops closed and must be repaired before rerunning. For an existing Builder delivery change, the first run emits `evolution_review_required` plus an audit path and SHA-256. Inspect the immutable audit and rerun the same command with that exact emitted SHA-256 and a concrete review reason; never calculate or substitute the hash. After any other failure, repair the model files (or the reported stage input) and re-run the same command. stdout carries one compact summary; the complete receipt is written to `.domainry/builder/model-apply.json`. `--sync-only` stops after the Ledger sync and checklist for local iteration without a Domainry Plane target.\n- `export` bootstraps model files from the current Ledger (one file per module) for a project created before this workflow; it refuses to overwrite existing model files without `--force`.\n\nCAS identities, batch contexts, and Ledger journals are handled entirely inside the CLI. Never compute, copy, or supply a SHA-256 by hand, and never call Domainry Plane directly or reconstruct its request signing.\n\n## Convergence and safety rules\n\n`plan`/`apply` classify every declared item against the current Ledger:\n\n- **create / update** — applied automatically and atomically; an unchanged item is never rewritten.\n- **delete** — an item present in the Ledger but absent from the model is destructive. `apply` refuses and names the missing items until the model restores them or the run explicitly passes `--allow-destructive`.\n- A **rename** of `module`, `kind`, or `key` is a delete plus a create. Treat renames as destructive: confirm data expectations first, then apply with explicit consent.\n- The model owns definitions only. Runtime instance data — business records, sessions, credentials, user-role assignments made at runtime — is never compared or mutated by convergence.\n\nComplete each module's full inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, seeds, acceptance) before the first `model validate`; do not validate a partial inventory and do not use `validate` or `apply` as a resource-by-resource schema probe. Immediately before the first validation, perform one cross-resource preflight over the whole draft: ownership closes **both ways** (every requirement behavior resolves to its resource, and every emitted Action/Workflow/Scheduler/Automation — including export/audit helper Actions — has one matching behavior owner); every Action's allowed roles have the required object access plus reads of every declared project relation target; Identity bootstrap/demo-user/profile references resolve; every frontend entrypoint object exists; Report SQL paths equal `backend/reports/<report-key>.sql`; export controls resolve their Report, Action, audit/download Objects, mappings, and one role's complete grant chain; every declared Object has a valid seed; and acceptance actors, roles, operations, positive cases, and denial cases resolve. Run validation only after this inventory is closed. On failure, collect and repair the complete diagnostic set and re-run once; do not revalidate after each local edit. Once validation returns `state:\"valid\"`, keep that model immutable while attaching evidence. Any requirement-backed edit invalidates the result and starts a new atomic `validate` + `plan` cycle. After a failed apply, fix the complete finding set in the files and re-run once.\n\n**Initial/default-state closure is part of that single preflight.** For every explicit requirement or accepted evidence statement that names an initial or default field value, author the value as the owning `objects[].fields[].default_value`. This is mandatory when the Object is exposed through generic Runtime create (including `entrypoints[].create_objects` or a role's `<object>.create` permission); acceptance prose, a seed, a frontend form default, or an Action input constant is not metadata default behavior. Mechanically verify that every select default matches its stable field option vocabulary and every state-field default matches both that vocabulary and the owning `state_machines[].states`. If the requirement does not identify an initial/default value, do not invent one merely to satisfy prose; record only the deterministic behavior the accepted demand actually establishes.\n\nBefore that first validation, apply this strict nested-shape card; these objects reject aliases and convenient prose fields:\n\n- `identity_bootstrap.menus[]` uses `id`, `key`, `label`, optional `description`/`route`/`icon`/`parent_id`, and required `sort_order` plus `status`; there is no `surface`. Bind roles with one row per pair, `role_menus:[{\"role_id\":\"technician\",\"menu_id\":\"menu_my_work\"}]`, or with `role_menu_sets:[{\"role_id\":\"technician\",\"menu_ids\":[\"menu_my_work\"]}]`; never use `role_key`, `menu_keys`, or a grouped object under `role_menus`.\n- Object field `validation` uses the exact Blueprint keys `min_length`, `max_length`, `min`, `max`, `pattern`, `options`, and `target`; `minimum`/`maximum` are Runtime authoring API names, not Blueprint JSON fields.\n- Every `expected_reports[].metrics` value is an array; use `[]` when the scenario asserts execution/export but no metric values. It is never an object map.\n- A Report emission repeats its identity inside the value: `{\"collection\":\"reports\",\"key\":\"part_usage_report\",\"value\":{\"key\":\"part_usage_report\",...}}`. The envelope `key` does not populate `value.key`; omitting the inner key makes `sql_file` resolve against an empty report key. The two keys must be identical.\n- For `object_sql_v1`, each SQL `JOIN ... AS <alias>` has exactly one `join_cardinalities` entry shaped `{\"alias\":\"<alias>\",\"cardinality\":\"many_to_one\"}` (or another truthful supported cardinality). It has only `alias` and `cardinality`; never use `left_alias`/`right_alias` there. Those names belong to Dataset join definitions, not Object SQL cardinality declarations.\n- `report_export_controls[]` is closed to the fields listed in [reports](capabilities/reports.md). Do not invent requester/scope convenience properties; requester scoping is enforced by the declared grant chain and record mapping.\n- `object_sql_v1` SQL has explicit `ORDER BY` and a literal business-bounded `LIMIT` in the inclusive range `1..10000`; the Blueprint `limit` diagnostic refers to SQL text, not a JSON `limit` property. Never use a larger placeholder such as `LIMIT 100000`.\n\nBusiness menu definitions and role-menu assignments are **not emitted collections**. They are deterministic project bootstrap facts under the single project-level `identity_bootstrap` object. When acceptance names `effective_menu_list`, declare the needed `identity_bootstrap.menus` and either `role_menus` or `role_menu_sets` before apply; authoring rejects a probed role with no binding. Do not postpone that acceptance prerequisite to a manual post-apply step. Runtime's Identity menu management API remains the supported path for later operational edits; updates keep their normal schema-hash CAS contract.\n\n`identity_bootstrap` (departments, organization scopes/memberships, workforce profiles/assignments, menus and role-menu bindings) is **not** an emits collection — an emitted collection always renders as an array and fails `schema.type: expected object`. It is a single project-level Blueprint property: declare it inside the **project item's `payload.blueprint.identity_bootstrap`** as `{\"version\":\"1\",\"departments\":[{\"id\":\"east\",\"name\":\"East\"}],\"menus\":[{\"id\":\"menu_my_work\",\"key\":\"my_work\",\"label\":\"My work\",\"route\":\"business.my_work\",\"sort_order\":10,\"status\":\"active\"}],\"role_menus\":[{\"role_id\":\"technician\",\"menu_id\":\"menu_my_work\"}]}`. `version` is the **string** constant `\"1\"` (integer `1` fails `schema.const`). `demo_users[].department_id` must reference a department `id` declared there (`identity.user_department_not_found` otherwise).\n\n`demo_users[].profile_bindings` is the single owner of a demo user's business-profile link. Its referenced profile seed must leave the profile extension's `identity_relation_field` absent; do not also write a user ID into that seed field. `model validate` rejects the two-owner form as `identity.user_profile_seed_relation_owned_twice`, before Runtime bootstrap. Runtime still converges the exact pre-existing same-user link idempotently for older valid deliveries, but a different-user replacement remains a conflict.\n\nTwo more Action/role rules the validator enforces:\n\n- **Every project-owned Handler Action — including scheduler-driven bulk handlers — must declare at least one required payload field in `idempotency_keys`** (`ledger.action_idempotency_required`). A scheduled job's invocation payload carries the key (e.g. a required `run_date` bound from the trigger event).\n- **Multiple `data_permissions[]` entries for the same object do NOT merge**: the validator reads one entry's `read`/`write` per object, so \"read=department + write=own\" cannot be expressed as two entries (`ledger.action_role_data_permission_required` reports the losing entry's booleans). Declare a single entry with the union capability and enforce the narrower write constraint through a before-update automation assert on the owner, a Handler precondition, or CRUD permission removal.\n\n## Single ownership\n\nEvery rendered resource key has exactly one originating model item. A `ledger.resource_duplicate` diagnostic means a second item attempted to emit an existing resource; it is never an overlay or merge mechanism. Repair it in the model files: remove the duplicate declaration and extend the item that already owns the resource. For example, a missing permission on an emitted role belongs in the item that already emits the role, not in a new `actor_role` item emitting the same role key.\n\nEvery behavior `resource_key` must resolve to the concrete resource owned by that behavior; the exact owner-to-emitted-collection mapping and resource-key rules live in [behavior owner decision](capabilities/behavior-owner-decision.md) — do not guess a collection from prose.\n\nAgent metadata is resource-owned in the same model: emit `skills`, `agents`, `agent_tasks`, `agent_service_principals`, and `agent_entrypoints` from separate `resource` items in dependency order, each linked to the requirement it satisfies. Never place these collections inside the project item. Read [Agents and Agent Tasks](capabilities/agents-and-agent-tasks.md) before authoring them; exact fields and references come from the live Blueprint Definition consumed by `model validate|plan|apply`.\n\n## Action declarations\n\nAn emitted `actions[]` entry requires `key`, `name`, `object_key`, `kind`, and `audit_event`. `kind` is the closed enum `bulk_operation`, `conditional_update`, `object_create`, `object_operation`, `record_delete`, `record_operation`, `record_restore`, `record_update`, or `transition_state`.\n\n**Kind selection for Handler Actions**: a project-owned Handler must NOT use kind `object_create` — that kind carries the system `record.create` owner, and an Action with both a system operation and a business handler owner is rejected at **Runtime bootstrap** (\"action has both system operation record.create and business handler owners\"); no static stage (validate/finalize/check/verify/package) catches it. Use **`object_operation`** for an object-level Handler command (creation included) and **`record_operation`** for a record-targeted one; a Handler that creates records does the create through its data capabilities.\n\nAuthorization splits by ownership and both halves are enforced at compile time (`ledger.runtime_manifest_invalid`):\n\n- A **project-owned Handler Action** (one that declares `handler`) uses the `authorization` block (`allowed_roles`, …).\n- A **system Action** (`transition_state` and every other non-Handler kind) must instead declare `permission` — it compiles to Runtime `requires_permission`. `authorization` is rejected on a system Action, and a missing `permission` is rejected too. Runtime executes a system Action for any role holding that permission, so a role that must not execute a guarded transition (for example, \"only managers close\") must not hold the declared permission — or the transition must be owned by a manager-only Handler Action instead.\n- **The `permission` key must be exactly two dot-segments: `<object_key>.<suffix>`** (e.g. `ticket.transition_close`, not `ticket.transition.close`). Runtime authorization re-derives the check as `<action.object_key>.<last-segment>`, so a three-segment key like `ticket.transition.start` passes `model validate` and one authorization layer but is re-read as the never-granted `ticket.start` by the second — every legitimate caller then fails with 403 `backend.permission.denied` at runtime. Platform permission keys granted verbatim in `roles[].permissions` (e.g. `identity.audit.view`) are exact-match strings and are not subject to this rule; it binds project-declared Action `permission` values and object CRUD keys.\n\nAcceptance scenarios are strict Blueprint resources, not free-form test notes. Use only the declared top-level fields `key`, `name`, `description`, `actor`, `role`, `role_key`, `seed_data`, `seed_records`, `actions`, `attempted_actions`, `workflows`, `expected_outcome`, `expected_error_code`, `expected_records`, `expected_reports`, `assertions`, and `config`; never invent shortcuts such as `objects` or `attempted_objects`. Express record/RLS expectations through `expected_records` or typed `assertions`.\n\n`workflows` means a positive **manual** Workflow invocation and therefore requires `trigger_contract.type:\"manual\"`. Do not list `scheduled`, `action_completed`, or `record_event` Workflows there: the finalizer attributes them to their real trigger owner (the matching `scheduled_jobs`, Action, or Automation operation) and rejects a fabricated manual-run case with `acceptance.workflow_manual_trigger_required`.\n\nThe scenario-level `expected_outcome` closed enum is `\"\"`, `positive`, `exception`, or `denied` — nothing else. Use `positive` for an expected success, `denied` for an expected Runtime authorization denial, and `exception` for every other expected non-success (state-machine rejection, RLS concealment, business precondition failure). Do not confuse it with the different `config.runtime_operations[].expected_outcome` enum documented below; the two enums are validated independently and values are not interchangeable. A `denied` scenario's role must actually be unable to execute every attempted Action: the composer rejects a denied scenario whose role holds the Action's authorization (`compose.acceptance_denied_role_can_execute_action`) — pick a genuinely unauthorized role or remove the grant.\n\n### Acceptance composition rules enforced by `project acceptance prepare`\n\nThe denominator builder enforces these composition rules; authoring scenarios without them costs one repair round each:\n\n- **Requirement coverage**: every `requirement` item — including purely declarative ones such as acceptance provisioning or navigation — must be reachable from at least one `acceptance` item's `requirement_keys` whose scenarios produce executable cases (`acceptance.requirement_coverage_missing: <key> resources=N operations=0 cases=0` otherwise). Cases come only from `actions`/`attempted_actions`, `workflows`, `expected_reports`, and `config.runtime_operations`; a scenario carrying only `expected_records` produces no case.\n- **Write denial coverage**: every write operation — each write Action and each write `runtime_operations` route — needs a denied-role case (`acceptance.permission_denial_missing`). For Actions, author a `denied` scenario with an unauthorized role. For typed routes, author a scenario whose `runtime_operations` entry sets **`\"case\": \"permission_denied\"`** (setting only `expected_outcome: denied` is not recognized); its default error code is `auth.permission_denied`, and its role must genuinely lack the route's required permissions.\n- **Handler positive coverage is symmetric and DIRECT**: every Handler Action also needs a positive scenario whose `actions` list references it directly with an allowed role (`ledger.action_positive_scenario_missing`). Coverage through a Workflow branch that invokes the Handler does not count — a scheduler-driven or approval-driven Handler still needs one direct positive probe (e.g. the operator role invoking it with an explicit payload).\n- **Every declared Object needs at least one seed** — including runtime-product objects such as reminder records or export audit/download artifacts (`ledger.seed_evidence_incomplete` names the missing objects); there is no exemption for objects normally populated only at runtime.\n- Treat seed records as a closed typed graph before apply: every required field needs a value or declared default, every `$record:<seed-key>` relation must target a seed of the relation's declared object, option values must belong to the field value domain, and undeclared fields are errors. `model validate` reports these at the exact seed field; do not defer them to Runtime bootstrap.\n- **Relation-typed `payload_fields` require `target_object_key`** referencing a **project** object; a Foundation target (e.g. an organization unit) is not resolvable there — pass the identifier as `text` and keep the Object field itself as the real relation.\n- **Denied report cases are auto-derived — never author them.** A scenario referencing a report in `expected_reports` must use a role that IS allowed to read it (`acceptance.report_role_not_allowed` otherwise). For a protected report (non-empty `audience_roles`/`required_permissions`), the builder automatically finds an unauthorized role and emits the `permission_denied` case itself; it fails `acceptance.report_denied_role_missing` if no declared role is unauthorized. The same auto-derivation applies to Workflow denied cases.\n- **CRUD/list/navigation coverage uses `config.runtime_operations` route keys** from the pinned Runtime Domain API `routes` map. The most-used keys: `object_query` (bounded list GET /objects/{objectKey}/records), `object_detail`, `object_create` (idempotency-key required), `object_update`, `object_delete`, `object_action`, `record_action`, `record_export`, `record_import_apply`, `effective_menu_list` (GET /identity/effective-menus), `business_notification_list`/`business_notification_stream` (SSE). Verify a key's readiness through `contract capability-category` before relying on it.\n- **Prepare prerequisites**: a current verification receipt, a running healthy managed Runtime of the exact current cohort, and the runtime-client SDK materialized. Pass `--project` as an **absolute path** — a relative path currently fails the database-identity check with `managed Runtime database identity escapes the current project`.\n\nTheir nested shapes are closed too. `expected_records[]` accepts only `object_key`, `record_key`, `field_key`, `state`, `count`, and `values`; it has no `scope`. `expected_reports[]` accepts only `report_key`, `expected_run`, `expected_export`, `filters`, and array-valued `metrics` (use `[]`, not `{}`, when no metric is asserted). Assertions accept only their published typed assertion fields.\n\nWhen a requirement consumes a typed Runtime capability that is not already represented by `actions`, `workflows`, or `expected_reports`, declare it under the scenario's `config.runtime_operations`. Each entry uses only `route_key` from the pinned Runtime Domain API plus optional `case`, `lifecycle`, `restart_sensitive`, `expected_outcome`, and `expected_error_code`; unknown fields, wrong field types, and route keys absent from the pinned Runtime Domain API fail during `model validate` (`evidence.typed_operation_route_unknown`). Use separate positive and `permission_denied` scenarios/roles for a write. The acceptance denominator resolves the route to the unified capability disclosure, preserves its real read/write effect class, required permissions, and SSE disposition, and rejects an allowed role labeled denied, a positive role missing the published permission, or a write without both positive and denied coverage. `expected_outcome`, when supplied, is one of `passed`, `denied`, `conflict`, `rejected`, `concealed`, `not_found`, or `failed`. For an SSE route, the case/lifecycle must be `sse_disconnect_resume`/`disconnect_resume` and `restart_sensitive` must be omitted or `false`: Last-Event-ID reconnect is not a managed process restart and requires only the stream transcript plus durable refetch. Do not reduce it to a connection-open check or attach unrelated restart/audit evidence.\n\n```json\n{\"config\": {\n  \"runtime_operations\": [\n    {\"route_key\":\"business_notification_stream\",\"case\":\"sse_disconnect_resume\",\"lifecycle\":\"disconnect_resume\"}\n  ]\n}}\n```\n\nA positive scenario's referenced seed records must already satisfy every source-owned Handler precondition needed for the expected success path. Every positive or denied role used by executable acceptance must have a project-declared `demo_users[]` identity. Bind a specific identity through `actor`; when an automatic standard case only has a role, that role must have exactly one demo user or exactly one user marked `acceptance_default:true`. The default applies across both `role_key` and `role_keys`; alphabetical order is never identity resolution. A role label alone is not a login. Keep separate negative business-precondition scenarios when that rejection matters. Denied authorization scenarios remain distinct and must fail at the Runtime authorization boundary.\n\nWhen a positive Action needs a value the standard runner cannot derive, declare the runner-owned fixture in that scenario's open `config` map: `\"action_payloads\":[{\"action_key\":\"<declared scenario action>\",\"fields\":{\"<payload key>\":{\"value\":<typed scalar>}}}]`. An omitted `encoding` requires a literal conforming exactly to the declared scalar payload type; relation/reference literals accept only `$record:<seed-key>` of the declared target, and user literals accept only `$actor`. The existing `{\"encoding\":\"json\",\"value\":<array or object>}` form remains available only for `text`/`long_text`, resolving nested `$record:<seed-key>` values before canonical JSON encoding. The Action must belong to that positive scenario, actor plus Action must identify one scenario/role binding, and malformed, missing-seed, duplicate, or ambiguous fixtures fail closed; fixture fields are never conflict-mutation slots. Do not declare fixtures for values the standard type derivation already supplies.\n\nValidation must close actor/role, object/relation, Action/authorization/data access, Automation/Workflow/Scheduler ownership, frontend entrypoints, and positive plus denied acceptance. Single-owner semantics and behavior shape tuples live in [behavior owner decision](capabilities/behavior-owner-decision.md).\n\n## Templates\n\n`templates/backend-model-handler-minimal/model.json.tmpl` is the directly loadable backend-model starter for a real two-object Handler transaction. Copy it to `backend/model/<module>.json`, replace only declared `{{...}}` decisions, and run `model validate`. The template is not the capability boundary: derive identity binding, custom RLS, behavior-owner paths, Surfaces, seeds, and acceptance from the routed capability documents plus bounded CLI context. Template usage and the composable `ledger-*` pattern packs are indexed in `templates/README.md`.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
