{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_17","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,340p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt70/skills/domainry-builder-v1/assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Development TODO\n\nAgent session ID:\n\nBatch:\n\nRequirement:\n\nProject mode: new / existing\n\nExecution scope: requirements_only / implementation\n\nBatch scale: full / light (light = existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; record the justification)\n\nSections omitted (light batch): none / one line per omitted subsection with what this batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference\n\nScope:\n\nFrontend mutation scope: integration_authorized / backend_only / externally_owned / unauthorized\n\nCurrent stage: requirements\n\nEvery checkbox is immediately followed by its own indented `Evidence:` line. Check an item only in the same edit that replaces `Evidence: pending` with concise proof for that exact item: command and result, source path/diff, artifact or receipt identity, Runtime/API/database record, or explicit user confirmation. A conclusion already written in the PRD or a matrix is cited as a pointer (`PRD §N`, `file:line`, receipt id), never restated; command+result evidence is still written out. Evidence file content must be produced by a command, script, or platform tool and be reproducible (recorded command line, or source plus sha256); never hand-transcribe, excerpt, or re-arrange content into an evidence file, and an `Evidence:` line carries only the gate ID and path pointers — never more than one inline line of content (rule authority: references/verification.md, Evidence rules). Execute check/finalize/test commands through the matching Gate `run` on their first real execution and use its returned `evidence_path`; never run once directly and again for evidence, search historical receipts, or use another command's receipt plus a note. Leave unfinished or failed work unchecked. A chapter-level evidence paragraph never justifies unexplained bulk checks.\n\nFrontend applicability is a machine determination, never self-reported: the project ships a frontend exactly when `frontend/src/**` contains at least one regular source file, and the deciding evidence records the exact `ls frontend/src` (or equivalent) command output. With no frontend, keep each section's single `frontend_not_present` closure item and delete every item marked `(only-if-frontend)` at instantiation; that one closure evidence per section covers the section's whole frontend group. With a frontend, delete the `frontend_not_present` closure items and keep every `(only-if-frontend)` item — none may be dropped or weakened.\n\nMaintain `.domainry/development/stages.json`: create it as `{}` at session start, then at each stage boundary record `{\"<stage>\": {\"started\": <epoch-seconds>, \"ended\": <epoch-seconds>}}` for stage ∈ `requirements` / `model` / `apply` / `implement` / `verify` / `acceptance` (write `started` on entering and `ended` on leaving the stage).\n\nExecute this document strictly from top to bottom. Do not start implementation or acceptance for a later in-scope item while an earlier prerequisite is unchecked, pending, open, or blocked. A validation round may continue safe checks only to collect its complete finding inventory; later completion items remain unchecked until earlier prerequisites close. If this template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, correct and validate the template before using or releasing it—never work around it by executing sections out of order.\n\nFor `requirements_only`, keep only the header, these rules, `requirements`, `domain truth design`, and `done`; record model/apply/Runtime/verify omission in the header and never claim delivery, integration, or L1-L5 implementation evidence. Bind Gate evidence with `--bind requirements` to the deterministic evidence closure: mandatory non-empty `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md`, root `requirements.md` when present, and the non-derived regular files under `frontend/**` when `frontend/src/**` proves a frontend exists. A frontend-led or immutable external-reference PRD may omit root `requirements.md`; it records the authority plus immutable snapshot/commit or citation, and the frontend closure is included automatically. Never guess `--bind source`. After `list` supplies the Gate ID, direct artifact closure uses `attach --gate-id <gate-id> --status passed --evidence-type source_artifact --evidence-path docs/backend-requirements-prd.md --bind requirements`, and validator closure uses `run --gate-id <gate-id> --bind requirements -- <validator argv>`. Validation commands use no pipeline or set `pipefail` inside the executed shell before the pipeline, so an internal failure cannot be masked by `tee`. For an implementation light batch, instantiate only the sections the batch touches per the batch-scale rules in development-guide.md (always keep `verify` and `done`; record every omission in `Sections omitted` with the prior accepted evidence reference; never omit or close as not applicable anything the batch touches; reclassify as full and copy the missing sections back if a light criterion breaks mid-batch).\n\nKeep the `(only-if-expected-invalid-check)` item only when the prepared source state makes the first `project check` genuinely expected to return a repair inventory; otherwise delete it. If that expectation becomes concrete after `project source prepare`, insert the item in its physical position immediately before the valid-check Gate before executing the check. Do not retroactively add it after a direct failed command.\n\n## requirements\n\n- [ ] frontend_not_present: machine check recorded no `frontend/src/**` regular source file (`ls frontend/src` output in the evidence file), so every `(only-if-frontend)` item in this section is not applicable\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] (only-if-frontend) Frontend inspected read-only across routes/pages, typed API, Mock adapters/fixtures, state/hooks, guards, tests, and relevant visible states; exact evidence paths and snapshot/commit recorded, with no Mock assumption promoted to business truth\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Full batch created or updated `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`, or light batch recorded a traceable PRD delta here with its accepted baseline reference\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Actors, journeys, object identities, fields/relations, queries, commands, states, permissions/scopes, errors/conflicts, calculations, transactions/idempotency, external effects, and frontend-only behavior extracted and classified as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] (only-if-frontend) Every route is dispositioned and every backend-bound typed API operation is traced to a requirement\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every write operation defines authorization, validation/errors, transaction boundary, idempotency/concurrency, durable result, and acceptance proof\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every acceptance journey has a semantic execution row in the PRD binding requirement, actor, formal Runtime identity/context source, fresh setup and dependencies, command/read surfaces, target-specific non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle with unchanged state, durable reconciliation, and restart/replay expectation\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Traceability matrix maps demand evidence (frontend evidence where a frontend exists, otherwise the requirements document) → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → metadata/source implementation → permission/data scope → acceptance journey\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every `business_blocker` names at least two concrete mutually incompatible choices, the explicitly required observable result/authorization/amount-unit/external-effect/state-flow difference, and only the smallest affected downstream model slice; missing optional metadata/preferences/mechanisms use nonblocking defaults or follow-ups\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Exact decimals, thresholds, and sums remain modelable without optional currency/unit/range metadata unless incompatible units change a required comparison/conversion; asynchronous approval pending can live on an independent approval Object; immutable resources need no invented business read actor; weekday/morning schedules use deterministic deployment timezone plus standard weekday cron when timezone/holiday preference is absent\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Requirement decisions and open questions\n\n| Decision ID | Affected requirements | Topic / conflict | Critical? | Classification | Decision owner | Confirmation / blocker | Excluded acceptance scope |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## domain truth design\n\n- [ ] Every calculation and report input is classified as an occurred fact, a summary of occurred facts, or a hypothetical simulation; simulations are isolated and never post into official reports or ledgers\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every material calculation closes Calculation Owner, Evaluation Time, Persisted Result, Policy/Rule Snapshot, Input Snapshot, Calculation Trace, Correction/Reversal Strategy, Downstream Consumers, and Report Source of Truth before capability discovery\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Historical financial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts use persisted event-time rules and inputs; no report, page, export, or payroll consumer joins current Settings to recompute history\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every locking write is assigned to an atomic Action; immutable snapshot/ledger creation, idempotency, and reversal/adjustment boundaries are confirmed in the same transaction\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Summary, detail, page, payroll, and export consumers use the same authoritative facts; missing facts remain explicit unknown/missing states and are never rendered as zero\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every Report that returns a list closes a bounded server-pagination contract: `page_size`/`cursor` or platform standard, maximum page size, stable ordering and cursor identity, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics; no unbounded or browser-memory pagination remains\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Canonical server fingerprint excludes `audit_id`; a new audit for the same conditions is linked/closed against the original job or valid artifact without 409, duplicate job/file/download, or duplicate terminal notification\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Canonical database compare-and-create proves concurrent replay, running replay, completed unexpired artifact replay, and new execution only after failed/cancelled/expired/corrupt or source/parameter/Scope change\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Domain truth and calculation ownership matrix\n\n| Requirement | Fact type | Calculation owner / evaluation time | Persisted result + policy/input snapshots + trace | Correction/reversal | Downstream consumers | Report source of truth | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## model\n\n- [ ] frontend_not_present: machine check recorded no `frontend/src/**` regular source file (`ls frontend/src` output in the evidence file), so every `(only-if-frontend)` item in this section is not applicable\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Metadata design\n\n- [ ] Delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and capability disclosure are present and valid; if absent, `\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \"$PROJECT_ROOT\"` succeeded against the pinned target, with no copied/fabricated SDK, alias, raw HTTP, or project BFF workaround\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Complete G01-G32 applicability matrix was derived from the current requirement/resource/operation inventory before model authoring; every applicable static contract shapes the model and every not-applicable family has a machine-derived reason\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n- [ ] Global demand pool derived from the requirements with each demand's source recorded; canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, scopes, and sensitive fields reconciled; every canonical resource has one owner module and all module inventories are complete\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] (only-if-frontend) Demand pool also derived from the delivered frontend's Mock interface contract: typed API layer and Mock adapters inspected read-only, each demand's source recorded\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] New-project local/test acceptance identities, Workforce profiles/assignments, departments/jobs/positions, and minimum real journey records are modeled before first backend publication, or existing-project identities are safely reusable\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every Runtime-owned identity, hierarchy value, principal claim, cursor, fingerprint, or other canonical context value has a formal typed-client/seed/admin/persisted-result source and is carried unchanged; no metadata, seed, Handler, or acceptance code guesses, injects, trims, pads, case-folds, or otherwise normalizes it outside a published Runtime transformation\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Identity menu entries and role-menu assignments are configured through foundation menu management\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, and external operations complete; every project-owned source gap is concrete\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every required platform/Admin capability was checked against `runtime-domain-api-v1.admin_business_reuse` before declaring a backend gap; candidate category keys were aggregated across all requirements and each selected category batch loaded exactly once with `\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\" contract capability-category --json --project <project> --key <fixed-category-key>` evidence\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every required operation maps to a returned endpoint or an explicit owner gap; unknown keys and free-text Admin capability search are absent; every `operations_only` capability remains disclosed as existing but isolated from Business Workspace; Runtime route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema are recorded separately; no capability was declared absent from a missing convenience wrapper alone\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Platform/Admin capability reuse matrix\n\n| Fixed category key | Fixed description / use case | Selected by requirements | Category batch receipt/SHA | Reusable count | Operations-only count |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | ---: | ---: |\n\n| Requirement | Required operation | Fixed category key | Capability and route | Reuse classification | Surface projection | Owner permission | Data scope | Machine schema | Gap/result |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## apply\n\n- [ ] frontend_not_present: machine check recorded no `frontend/src/**` regular source file (`ls frontend/src` output in the evidence file), so every `(only-if-frontend)` item in this section is not applicable\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Backend model and materialization\n\n- [ ] Every module's complete inventory was declared in `backend/model/*.json` before the first Gate-owned `model preflight`; each repair-required Gate locator was used to read `.domainry/builder/disposable/model-preflight-result.json` directly, its complete diagnostic batch was repaired without bespoke jq/regex validation or a diagnostic-recovery rerun, local preflight reached valid before canonical `model validate`, and the first valid draft stayed frozen unless a requirement-backed edit started a new preflight-plus-validate-plus-plan cycle\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Before the first `model validate`, strict nested shapes are checked: exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, field `min`/`max`, array report metrics; every Report emission repeats its envelope key as `value.key`, every Object SQL join cardinality is exactly `{alias,cardinality}`, and SQL has a literal `LIMIT` in `1..10000`; export controls have one complete role grant chain; behavior/resource ownership closes both ways\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Model files grouped by owning module, each `module/kind/key` declared exactly once, `model preflight` passes locally, and canonical `model validate` passes\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] `model plan` classification reviewed: creates/updates expected, and no unexplained destructive delete (any `--allow-destructive` run records its reviewed item list)\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every failed `model apply` was repaired in the model files and the same command re-run; no hand edit of `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Aggregated checklist diagnostics repaired; live validation passes inside apply\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Release completed once through the single `model apply` command with its receipt at `.domainry/builder/model-apply.json`; backend materialization created, modified, or deleted no `frontend/**` path\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Batch implementation and integration\n\n- [ ] `project source prepare` inventoried every delivered Action and created every missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeleton without replacing existing source; all prepared placeholders were subsequently implemented and none is used as positive behavior evidence\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] (only-if-expected-invalid-check) Expected-invalid `project check` diagnostic inventory captured on its first and only pre-repair execution with expected exit 1, contract `domainry-project-check-v1`, state `invalid`, and the exact bounded diagnostic code set; the receipt retains the product exit and complete structured inventory\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every implemented Handler's generated placeholder test was replaced with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs and current result/effect assertions; `project check --json --scope actions` exits 0 with `state:\"valid\"` before every `project source finalize` and before any developer Go test — check/finalize share the stale-scaffold invariant, and finalize also shares the applicable production-Action Go compile invariant\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Handler/domain/Connector source implemented by module, and the current local composition finalized after source implementation and before developer-run tests\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Module-focused Go tests pass with a command receipt whose exact argv is `go test` (use Gate `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`)\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Cross-module tests pass\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] One highest-risk semantic canary passed against the managed Runtime before broad integration: formal identity/context → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back, with exact target/non-empty positive proof, intended same-record scope/error contrast, and unchanged denied/conflict state; dependent journeys remained blocked until its root cause closed\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Conditional-mutation static self-check exits 0 (run the self-check command from `references/runtime-client.md`, Conditional mutation section): every project-owned `.ConditionalUpdate(` call site carries a generated condition or arithmetic effect, covering call sites no unit case exercises\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Complete backend/Runtime integration round validates real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every in-scope Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint/Service Principal and Workflow `agent_task` node is model-owned and validated; allowlist intersection, inherited/service identity, Business route access, operations-only isolation, durable task state, Tool/Action receipts, denial, retry/cancel/recovery, Workflow resume, and Runtime-restart persistence have explicit acceptance rows\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every integration mismatch reconciled against the explicit requirement, the published Runtime contract, the typed frontend API and Mock/tests where a frontend exists, and the real response; the actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner is recorded, and no backend/Runtime gap is hidden by a compatibility layer, fallback, raw HTTP, or weakened test\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] (only-if-frontend) Every frontend- or Mock-owned problem is recorded as a finding row with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, then closed as `resolved: recorded_for_frontend_owner` — never repaired: `frontend/**` is not modified under any recorded mutation scope, and a finding that truly blocks a backend journey marks that journey explicitly blocked rather than mislabeled as an owner gap\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Authorization and navigation data acceptance\n\nA light batch with no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change omits this whole section at instantiation and records the omission in the header; if the section is already instantiated, close every item as not applicable with scope evidence referencing the prior accepted matrices and totals, and leave the two matrices empty with that reference.\n\n- [ ] Real Runtime seed and business database data accepted before matrix capture, and the published Action total recorded\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Action authorization matrix rows follow delta scope: for a full batch, exactly one row per published Action; for an existing-project increment, one row per Action added or changed in this batch plus summary rows referencing the prior accepted matrix evidence, together covering the published total\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Every authored row has a published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe that observes the exact target and non-empty expected facts, and one denied probe that reaches the intended stable concealment/error category with unchanged durable state against the same Runtime data\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Persisted roles, menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and relevant permission state inspected in the actual project database\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Agent-created/reused real Runtime sessions loaded `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace` for every application role affected by this batch through project-provisioned non-human acceptance identities, and the response reconciles with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments, with unchanged roles referencing prior accepted evidence; no acceptance step asks the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Authorization and navigation totals\n\n| Published Actions | Action matrix rows (authored + referenced) | Application roles | Roles queried | Persisted role-menu assignments | Effective menu entries | Mismatches |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\n\n### Action authorization matrix\n\n| Action | Target/Object | Kind | Permission / allowed roles | Required data scope | Allowed positive control (exact target + non-empty facts) | Denied same-record contrast (exact outcome + unchanged state) | Runtime/DB evidence | Prior evidence ref | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n### Role effective-menu matrix\n\n| Role | Persisted menu assignments | Effective menus returned | Missing/unexpected | DB/API evidence | Prior evidence ref | Status |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n### Acceptance and convergence\n\n- [ ] Complete backend/Runtime integration round (including the frontend contract surface where a frontend exists) finished before repair, with the complete owner-classified finding inventory recorded\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Platform, metadata, shared integration, and module findings repaired; frontend-owned findings stay recorded for the frontend owner, never repaired here; the acceptance round passes and no open finding remains\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Integration findings\n\n| Round | ID | Owner (`frontend` / `backend` / `runtime` / shared) | Finding | State (`open` / `resolved`) | Evidence / resolution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## verify\n\n`project verify` and packaging establish L1 `delivery_verified` structural/release closure only. They do not establish L3 `scenario_verified`; every completed item below requires current project-local evidence bound by the development Gate to the applicable source/model/Runtime identity.\n\n- [ ] Git diff and source boundary reviewed with proof that `frontend/**` (where present) received no delivery-agent modification under any recorded mutation scope; backend changes share the current Gate source SHA\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Backend formatting passes\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Backend lint passes\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Backend vet passes\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Backend focused tests pass\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Backend full tests pass\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Current metadata checklist and live validation pass; project verification passes for the current finalized source with its complete passing check inventory, recorded as `delivery_verified` only and never used as real-data `scenario_verified` evidence\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Project packaging passes for the current verified source with its package receipt and artifact summary bound to the current Runtime manifest, recorded as `delivery_verified` only and never used as real-data `scenario_verified` evidence\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Checklist, source-finalization, and project-verification failure receipts were reviewed as complete `passed/failed/skipped_dependency` inventories; every failed row was repaired and every dependency-skipped row executed in the passing rerun\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Frozen acceptance inventory is the current healthy managed Runtime-bound denominator, CLI-generated and hashed from requirement IDs, model resources, typed operations, roles/data scopes, capability lifecycles, model/Blueprint/capability identities, and the exact Runtime cohort; its `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1` maps every case to one actor, formal principal context, fresh setup, command, fact closure, and reason-specific oracle, and the CLI-owned denominator is not hand-edited\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Managed Runtime start executed once for the current project-relative package bundle on an explicit loopback address; the v3 managed process receipt proves the exact current delivery, binary, source, Runtime-manifest, and database cohort identities\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Current managed Runtime health is running, current, and HTTP-healthy on its bound loopback address; `runtime-process status` revalidates the same v3 managed process, delivery, binary, Runtime-manifest, and database cohort identities\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Matching managed Runtime and exact delivery/manifest identity are healthy; project-provisioned non-human acceptance sessions and differentiated scope data are ready without interactive user login; self-built harness code and intermediates live under `.domainry/development/**` from the first file, never under `backend/**` (dirtying the finalized source makes every receipt stale and forces a full finalize/verify/package redo plus evidence re-record)\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Acceptance executed in order: `project acceptance prepare` → `project acceptance run --cases standard` (runner executes the mechanical families first and writes one exact-set `domainry-backend-acceptance-results-v2` document containing passed/gap/typed-not-passed outcomes; its separate run report is diagnostics only; runner-passed cases are never re-implemented or overwritten; every non-business `not_passed` row is repaired at its named owner) → self-built business harness ONLY for results rows with `disposition:\"needs_business_harness\"`, written as assertion functions on top of `acceptance-harness-core.py` copied into `.domainry/development/` → `project acceptance check`; after any model evolution, `project acceptance stale-report` ran before any re-recording and only its `stale`+`missing` cases were re-recorded (an indiscriminate full re-record with a stale-report available is prohibited; non-zero `carried_forward_cases` in the check receipt is normal hash-proven coverage, not a gap)\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Complete operation x role x required-case denominator passes against the real Runtime; Agent cancel/retry/reconcile, SSE disconnect/resume with durable refetch, async-job restart/resume, allow/deny, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, error/conflict, durable effects, unchanged denied/error state, idempotent replay, and persistence read-back reach L3; `project acceptance check` proves exact set equality and rejects missing/orphan/self-reported evidence\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Acceptance scenarios use `harness.session_for_case(case)`, `ScenarioGraph`, and `ReasonOracle`: Runtime `GET /identity/principal-context` supplies every canonical selector, fresh per-scenario identities/records isolate setup, a positive case names and observes its target with required non-empty facts, a negative case reaches its intended stable status/error/concealment and proves unchanged state, and `setup_failed`/`blocked_dependency` cannot be recorded as a downstream product pass or failure\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Rule-version switch, cross-midnight attribution, multi-party split remainder, refund/reversal, post-lock correction, and cross-store/cross-entity authorization pass where applicable; every rule-change scenario proves historical non-drift\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Summary/detail/page/payroll/export results reconcile by immutable ledger ID and authoritative fact; scopes match, missing facts are not zero-filled, and no consumer repeats the formula\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] List Report acceptance proves maximum-page enforcement, deterministic next-page traversal without duplicates/gaps, and the declared `truncated`/`total` semantics under concurrent or equal-sort-key data\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Export acceptance proves the permission-filtered bounded threshold probe of the page's same Report under immutable parameters and authorization/data Scope: exhausted `0/1000` probes route to the exact-total governed synchronous CSV and an observed row 1001 to the asynchronous export job whose terminal artifact records the final exact total, with no separate count/full-result query; sync and async fingerprint replay reuses work without duplicate artifacts; only async completion/failure publishes requester Inbox linked to audit/job/artifact; governed download reauthorizes; and typed query-profile evidence proves database-bounded pages with no repeated full-result execution per chunk\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Active Runtime evidence confirms Report `page_size`/opaque signed cursor/`truncated`/`total_semantics`, stale-cursor rejection, 202 requester-owned export job status/cancel, shared batch checkpoint/retry, terminal exact total, Inbox linkage, and governed download routes; any older-package delta is assigned to the Runtime Report owner and is not disguised as project metadata\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] A real `1001`-row export on the current managed Runtime/database cohort (`database_driver` from the managed process receipt) prepared by a project user with a persisted role and non-empty data scope completes through the worker and artifact download; isolation is proven by application-layer data scope on that same acceptance database (no database-engine RLS posture is required — see G14); worker reauthorization restores workspace plus current Identity/business-principal facts, while genuine assignment/scope drift fails closed\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, outbox, and external-effect claims reach their declared terminal observable result; intermediate queued/waiting/emitted states are not counted as success\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Agent claims reach their declared terminal result with pinned definition/task identity and current authorization evidence; Agent analysis/proposal/action modes, own-run visibility, Workspace isolation, operations-only isolation, Tool/Action receipts, retry/cancel/reconciliation, Workflow wait/resume, and provider identity are proven without treating a deterministic runner as real-provider delivery\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Managed Runtime restart preserves sessions and durable state, and every restart-sensitive critical journey is rerun successfully at L5\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Final finding inventory is empty; temporary resources cleaned\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Evidence Gate status for `verify` passes with no stale, failed, pending, accepted-limitation, or open-finding blocker; TODO checker passes for the verify stage (`--expected-stage verify`)\n  - Evidence: pending\n\n### Verify findings\n\n| Round | ID | Owner | Finding | State (`open` / `resolved`) | Evidence / resolution |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n## done\n\nFor `requirements_only`, this section closes only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and reports no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. For `implementation`, it follows the terminal verification policy.\n\n- [ ] Terminal state is exactly `done`; all in-scope requirements and preceding stages have current bound evidence at their required level\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] No open finding remains and no requirement remains hidden, downgraded, or deferred\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] Completion summary reports delivery identity, tests, Runtime result, highest evidence level, restart result, and explicitly non-blocking limitations without claiming above the evidence reached\n  - Evidence: pending\n- [ ] `Current stage: done` is set; evidence Gate `done` status and the lightweight TODO consistency check both pass immediately before the final response\n  - Evidence: pending\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,340p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt70/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/production-technical-gates.md\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_18","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"sed -n '1,340p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt70/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/production-technical-gates.md\"","aggregated_output":"# Production technical gates\n\nRead this file completely before model authoring, source implementation, Runtime integration, or terminal verification. These gates cover backend and the backend-to-frontend Runtime Client boundary. They do not assess page layout or visual quality.\n\n## Gate semantics\n\nA gate is `applicable`, `not_applicable`, or `failed`; it is never silently skipped. `not_applicable` requires a machine-derived reason tied to the current model and capability inventory. An applicable gate passes only with both its declared static proof and its real Runtime proof. Static validation can reject a structurally unsafe candidate, but cannot prove transaction rollback, cross-request idempotency, concurrent correctness, query boundedness, tenant isolation, worker recovery, SSE resume, or external-provider terminal effects.\n\nThe complete denominator contains the 32 gate families below. A project may have multiple cases for one family. The acceptance denominator must bind every case to the current model, Blueprint, capability disclosure, generated source, Runtime Client package, packaged Runtime binary, and database cohort identity. Any bound identity change makes the evidence stale. (Isolation is proven behaviorally and database-agnostically per G14; no engine-specific security posture is bound or required.)\n\nEvidence may redact secrets and personal data, but must retain the route or operation, actor/role, workspace, request/idempotency/correlation identity, status or stable error code, state transition, cursor or job identity, and durable before/after digests needed to prove the case. A prose claim, test name, arbitrary file, 2xx response, `queued`, `waiting`, or self-reported percentage is not evidence.\n\n## A. Contract and delivery closure\n\n### G01 Requirement-to-operation closure\n\n- Applies when any executable requirement exists.\n- Model/static: every requirement behavior resolves to its owning resource, typed operation, allowed and denied actor, data scope, fixture, expected terminal effect and acceptance case. Every executable resource has the inverse link back to a requirement and at least one operation/case.\n- Runtime: exercise each changed operation and reference current prior evidence only for unchanged operations.\n- Blocks: orphan requirements/resources, a single generic case used to cover several distinct operations, or model inventory omitted from the denominator.\n\n### G02 Capability disclosure parity\n\n- Applies to every Business/Portal endpoint selected by the project.\n- Model/static: route policy, OpenAPI operation/schema, Runtime Domain API, Runtime Client method, owner, permission, scope, idempotency and stream semantics are an exact set. Static status is named `contract_ready`; it must not imply configured or scenario-verified behavior.\n- Runtime: prove the selected operation is enabled and executable in the current delivery; provider-backed capabilities also require provider verification.\n- Blocks: undocumented routes, catalog-only routes reported as scenario ready, generic request fallback presented as a typed client implementation, or stale capability hashes.\n\n### G03 Skill, source, documentation and binary parity\n\n- Applies to every release of this Skill.\n- Model/static: Skill command examples, Go dispatcher, repository binary, packaged binary, supported command list, version, capability names and contract hashes match. Every fenced/inline `Correct`, `minimal`, and pattern example is machine parsed and validated.\n- Runtime: packaged binary, not only the Go `run()` function, executes version/help and mandatory command smoke tests.\n- Blocks: a documented command absent from the shipped binary, contradictory capability claims, stale examples, or installed-package evidence used for a different source tree.\n\n### G04 One frontend intent to one Runtime command\n\n- Applies to every user intent that changes business state.\n- Model/static: the call-site inventory maps one intent to exactly one Runtime Action/command entrypoint. The Action payload carries all data needed to complete the intent; project BFFs, raw HTTP and client-side write orchestration are forbidden.\n- Runtime: one interaction produces one governed request identity and one terminal receipt. Internal typed capability calls may be multiple, but remain inside the single Runtime-owned work unit.\n- Blocks: open-table/open-shift/payment/submit flows implemented as sequential create/update calls, client rollback, a second repair call, or refetch presented as another business command.\n\n## B. Command correctness and consistency\n\n### G05 Single command boundary\n\n- Applies to every multi-record, state-transition, settlement or external-effecting intent.\n- Model/static: one project-owned Action or published Runtime System Operation owns validation, authorization, reads, writes and terminal output. It declares complete involved objects and effect sets.\n- Runtime: request and audit traces show exactly one command invocation; retries reuse the same idempotency identity.\n- Blocks: partial REST choreography, multiple endpoint calls required for success, or split authorization decisions.\n\n### G06 Atomic transaction and rollback\n\n- Applies when one command writes more than one record/table or couples a business write with audit/outbox/receipt state.\n- Model/static: read/write sets close over Handler capabilities and the contract requires one Runtime-owned unit of work. Project source cannot open or commit its own database transaction.\n- Runtime: inject failure after each write boundary and before commit; all business mutations, receipt, audit and outbox state roll back together. A retry produces one complete terminal result.\n- Blocks: best-effort audit/outbox, artifact-first multi-step completion without repair, or tests that assert only the success response.\n\n### G07 Idempotency and exact fingerprinting\n\n- Applies to every externally reachable mutation and every retryable worker/provider effect.\n- Model/static: a required stable idempotency identity is declared; fingerprints use normalized exact JSON values and include semantic preconditions while excluding secrets/volatile transport values.\n- Runtime: missing key is rejected, same key/same semantic payload replays the original receipt across restart, same key/different payload conflicts, concurrent duplicates execute once, and commit-unknown is reconciled.\n- Blocks: invocation-ID fallback for public HTTP, float64 conversion of money/large integers, pre-normalization claim collisions, or retry generating a new key.\n\n### G08 Concurrency, compare-and-set and fencing\n\n- Applies to mutations of existing records, capacity/inventory/balance changes, leases and asynchronous jobs.\n- Model/static: each existing-record write declares either lock-before-write (`get_for_update`) or conditional/CAS update; leases carry owner, token and expiry fencing.\n- Runtime: simultaneous competing commands prove the invariant, exactly one allowed transition where required, stable conflict errors, no lost update, and stale workers cannot commit.\n- Blocks: read-then-unconditional-update, unstable retry loops, fixed leases without a completion/fencing policy, or merely serial unit tests.\n\n## C. Domain and data integrity\n\n### G09 Exact quantities, money and ratios\n\n- Applies to currency, money, price, cost, fee, tax, balance, points, quantities whose scale matters, rates, ratios and percentages.\n- Model/static: exact named types, currency/unit, scale and rounding owner are declared. Action input/output `currency`, `decimal`, and `percent` fields reuse a named Object source field and inherit its exact contract. Project Action/domain source cannot use binary floating point or ignore parse/range errors for these values.\n- Runtime: boundary, rounding, negative/zero, maximum precision, replay and aggregation cases match the frozen rule snapshot.\n- Blocks: `float64` money, silently defaulting parse failures to zero, string formatting as calculation, or current Settings used to recalculate historical results.\n\n### G10 Historical truth, snapshots and corrections\n\n- Applies to settlement, billing, commission, payroll, inventory valuation, points or other historical results.\n- Model/static: policy identity/version, input snapshot, ordered calculation trace, result and immutable ledger entries are persisted; correction is reversal/adjustment, not mutation.\n- Runtime: changing current Settings does not alter old results; refund/cancel/correction appends linked entries and preserves net invariants.\n- Blocks: recomputation from current configuration, editable settled facts, or missing provenance.\n\n### G11 Database constraints and physical schema\n\n- Applies to every durable Object/table and relation.\n- Model/static: not-null, unique, foreign-key/check constraints and indexes reflect the business invariant; index definitions include ordered columns, uniqueness, direction and predicate where relevant. Reconciliation compares physical definitions, not names only.\n- Runtime: migrations succeed on the current managed Runtime/database cohort from clean and upgrade states (migration definitions stay dialect-portable across supported drivers); constraint violations return stable errors without partial writes.\n- Blocks: application-only uniqueness, missing tenant prefix in uniqueness/indexes, or failed introspection treated as an empty healthy state.\n\n### G12 Audit, outbox and receipt correlation\n\n- Applies to every mutation, workflow/job transition, external effect and privileged read/export.\n- Model/static: the contract declares audit event, receipt identity, correlation chain and after-commit/outbox owner. Terminal states distinguish intent, accepted, sent, delivered, reconciled and failed.\n- Runtime: request ID to invocation/action/workflow/job/outbox/provider/notification IDs is traversable; failure/restart produces one consistent terminal chain.\n- Blocks: best-effort audit loss, `queued` called delivered, replay returning an artifact whose audit/download chain is incomplete, or secrets in evidence.\n\n## D. Security and tenancy\n\n### G13 Credentials, sessions and tokens\n\n- Applies to authenticated deployments.\n- Model/static: no fixed/default production credential; bootstrap uses invitation or cryptographically random one-time credentials with forced change/expiry. Refresh consume-and-rotate is atomic and detects reuse.\n- Runtime: fresh production-like database rejects documented/default passwords; concurrent refresh permits one successor; revoke/restart/workspace mismatch and token-family reuse behave correctly.\n- Blocks: hard-coded `123456`, ignored configured password policy, non-atomic refresh rotation or fail-open lockout state.\n\n### G14 Authorization, tenant scope and row-level isolation\n\n- Applies to multi-tenant or scoped business data.\n- **Isolation is enforced at the application layer and is database-agnostic.** Tenant isolation is the `workspace_id` boundary carried by every row and applied to every query; business row scope is the declared `data_permissions` scope/predicate evaluated against actor claims. This holds identically on every supported dialect. Do NOT depend on, require, prove, or claim any database-engine feature — in particular PostgreSQL row-level security (`relrowsecurity`, `BYPASSRLS`, forced-policy catalogs, per-connection `set_config`) — for isolation; the framework must stay portable across databases, so engine-level RLS posture is never a required capability, evidence kind, or `done` precondition.\n- Model/static: least-privilege role and data scope are explicit; every scoped Object has a declared `data_permissions` entry and no scope relies on a database-engine policy.\n- Runtime: at least two workspaces and differentiated actors with non-empty colliding data prove allowed access and cross-tenant/cross-scope denial through ordinary HTTP, Action, Report, export, worker and direct-query probes on the current managed Runtime database cohort.\n- Blocks: empty-result tests passed off as isolation proof, role-header simulation, or any requirement for engine-specific RLS catalogs/posture as evidence.\n\n### G15 Field security and assurance\n\n- Applies to sensitive fields, high-risk Actions, exports or approval ceremonies.\n- Model/static: read/mask/hide/write/export policy and Action output lineage are declared; enhanced assurance has a real issuer and binds workspace, user, Action, record, version and payload digest.\n- Runtime: non-empty distinguishable sensitive values remain protected across Record, Action record/output, Report and export; missing/expired/replayed/mismatched assurance causes no mutation.\n- Blocks: Action output bypassing field policy, empty sensitive fixtures, frontend-only approval, or `normal_login` used as enhanced assurance.\n\n### G16 Hostile input, files, SQL, connectors and Agent tools\n\n- Applies when the capability accepts files, URLs, Object SQL, connector input or anonymous/task capabilities.\n- Model/static: parser-safe allowlists, size/type limits, clean-file receipts, SSRF/DNS/redirect rules and signed single-use Agent tool capabilities are explicit.\n- Runtime: traversal/symlink/MIME deception, quarantine/scan failure, SQL CTE/subquery/UNION/comment/timeout, private/loopback/link-local/IPv6/DNS-rebinding URLs, and forged/cross-tenant/replayed Agent tool calls fail closed.\n- Blocks: regex-only SQL rewriting, raw storage access, anonymous exception without negative proof, or emulator evidence presented as provider security.\n\n## E. Query and resource performance\n\n### G17 Execution-level bounded pagination\n\n- Applies to every list/report/search with data-dependent cardinality.\n- Model/static: API declares page size, opaque cursor, stable order and count semantics. Repository/executor accepts page/cursor and pushes `LIMIT page_size+1` or equivalent keyset/aggregate paging to the data owner. A result `limit` is only an output cap, not pagination proof.\n- Runtime: first/middle/last pages at target volume show bounded rows read, query count, latency and peak memory; no full materialization precedes slicing.\n- Blocks: fixed first 200/1000 rows, an Action `List` call without an explicit bounded page, full Summary followed by `paginate`, OFFSET traversal without a hard result bound plus a query-profile budget captured on the current managed Runtime database cohort, or async worker re-running the full Report for every page.\n\n### G18 Stable traversal under change\n\n- Applies to mutable paginated datasets.\n- Model/static: every sort appends a unique deterministic tie-breaker and fixes null/collation behavior. The signed cursor either encodes the complete keyset tuple or binds an authorized source-version snapshot to a bounded offset; the latter must reject continuation after source drift.\n- Runtime: equal sort keys plus concurrent insert/update/delete produce no duplicate or missing rows within the documented snapshot semantics; a source-version-fenced traversal returns a stable stale-cursor conflict rather than mixed-version rows, and retrying an unchanged cursor is deterministic.\n- Blocks: custom row sort without an ID/unique tie-breaker, grouped sort without every grouping tie term, page-number-only traversal, unsigned response index, or offset continuation after source drift.\n\n### G19 Index, query-plan and N+1 budget\n\n- Applies to growth-sensitive reads, imports, exports, principal construction and relation resolution.\n- Model/static: queries declare access pattern and expected composite/search index; loop-contained I/O requires a bounded exemption or batch interface.\n- Runtime: the acceptance database uses its driver-equivalent structured plan (`EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` for SQLite, `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON` for MySQL, `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON)` for PostgreSQL) and binds that plan format/dialect to the managed process receipt. Machine-checked access classification confirms index-driven execution on growth-sensitive paths; SQL statement/row-count observation at 1/10/100/1000 scale stays within rows-read and query-count budgets. Small-table full scans are accepted only when the bounded dataset fits one page and the plan records that scan explicitly.\n- Blocks: index-name-only checks, B-tree claimed for `%substring%`, per-membership/profile/row queries, or latency logs without thresholds.\n\n### G20 Timeouts, capacity, memory, streaming and fairness\n\n- Applies to external I/O, database work, workers, imports/exports and multi-tenant shared resources.\n- Model/static: request/job deadlines, byte/row/CPU/attempt/concurrency limits, cancellation, streaming/chunk ownership and per-tenant fairness semantics are declared.\n- Runtime: slow SQL/provider/worker cancellation stops downstream work, returns the connection/lease, prevents late writes, bounds RSS and keeps a cold tenant progressing beside a hot tenant.\n- Blocks: context timeout not propagated to DB, full artifact reassembly, process-local limits called tenant-global, missing job deadline, or unbounded goroutines.\n\n## F. Asynchronous and external lifecycle\n\n### G21 Workflow, scheduler and automation terminal semantics\n\n- Applies when those resources are modeled or used by a journey.\n- Model/static: triggers, branches, retries, dead letters, missed-window policy, run-as identity, before/after transaction boundary and terminal states are complete.\n- Runtime: process/node/task/action effects and audit all terminate correctly across retry, cancellation, permission drift and restart; before failure rolls back and after-commit work is durable.\n- Blocks: HTTP enqueue alone, waiting state called success, or schedule receipt not linked to Workflow/Action effect.\n\n### G22 Connector and provider effect reconciliation\n\n- Applies to any external provider call.\n- Model/static: connection/provider identity, operation DTOs, timeout/retry safety, idempotency, outbox, response reference, callback/reconciliation and compensation are declared.\n- Runtime: real configured provider evidence proves effect and reconciliation; retry/restart does not duplicate it, and uncertain outcomes remain explicitly reconcilable.\n- Blocks: deterministic emulator as provider acceptance, SMTP accepted as delivered, or synchronous external call while holding database locks.\n\n### G23 Worker lease, deadline and restart recovery\n\n- Applies to asynchronous jobs, exports/imports, report workers, Agent tasks and background reconciliation.\n- Model/static: durable queue, bounded claim, heartbeat, fencing token, deadline, attempts, terminal/dead-letter state and cleanup SLA exist.\n- Runtime: kill the old PID during each critical window, prove a new PID with the same database/delivery recovers once, stale owner cannot commit, and no item is lost or duplicated.\n- Blocks: in-memory queue, PID-less restart evidence, lease without fencing, or a worker test double.\n\n### G24 Notification and after-commit delivery\n\n- Applies when user/system notification is part of the accepted journey.\n- Model/static: event identity, audience, template, durable inbox/outbox state, channel semantics and retry policy are bound to the owning terminal business event.\n- Runtime: after commit/restart/replay, Inbox and channel effects appear once with correct actor/workspace visibility; rollback produces none.\n- Blocks: direct pre-commit send, duplicate terminal notification, template-only evidence, or channel failure hidden by Inbox success.\n\n## G. SSE and Agent lifecycle\n\n### G25 SSE identity, resume and durable refetch\n\n- Applies to every disclosed stream.\n- Model/static: event type, stable event ID, cursor/`Last-Event-ID`, retention/resync contract, authorization and durable refetch endpoint are published. Invalidation is not durable data.\n- Runtime: disconnect after a known event, reconnect with cursor, cover retained and expired cursors, restart and slow consumer; refetch returns the durable terminal truth without missing or duplicating effects.\n- Blocks: accepted/result events without IDs, client with no reconnect path, connect-only tests, or process memory presented as durable replay.\n\n### G26 Agent authorization and provider identity\n\n- Applies when Agent/agent-task capability is claimed.\n- Model/static: allowed tools/effect sets, service principal, workspace/role/scope recovery, provider configuration, model/policy and anonymous capability exceptions are explicit.\n- Runtime: a real configured provider returns external run/model/usage identity; forged/escalated/cross-workspace tools are denied and source handlers remain inside frozen capabilities.\n- Blocks: deterministic response fixture as Agent acceptance, bearer role simulation, or generic tool proxy.\n\n### G27 Agent retry, cancellation, reconciliation and receipts\n\n- Applies to asynchronous or tool-using Agent runs.\n- Model/static: task/run/tool-call identities, lease/fencing, retry safety, cancel semantics, partial tool effects and reconciliation are declared.\n- Runtime: cancel/retry/worker loss/provider timeout/restart produce one terminal task, durable tool receipts and reconciled business effects.\n- Blocks: UI cancellation only, retry creating a new uncontrolled task, or final text without tool/effect receipts.\n\n### G28 Multi-instance event/session durability\n\n- Applies when more than one Runtime replica or failover is claimed.\n- Model/static: session, rate limit, event backplane, cursor and worker coordination owners are shared or explicitly instance-local with limited claims.\n- Runtime: requests/retries/streams cross replicas, one replica dies, and authorization, limits, fan-out, resume and work ownership remain correct.\n- Blocks: process-local backplane/limiter advertised as global, sticky-session assumption without contract, or single-instance evidence used for HA.\n\n## H. Operations and acceptance integrity\n\n### G29 Observability, redaction and alert recovery\n\n- Applies to production deployment.\n- Model/static: low-cardinality metrics, structured logs, trace/correlation fields, redaction policy, queue/worker/provider/database indicators and actionable alerts are defined.\n- Runtime: success and injected failure show the complete correlation chain; secrets are absent; lag/retry/dead-letter/capacity alerts fire and recover.\n- Blocks: dashboard JSON or metric name existence alone, rows iteration omitted from SQL timing, or unbounded tenant/user labels.\n\n### G30 Failure-window and commit-unknown injection\n\n- Applies to every multi-step durable or external-effect lifecycle.\n- Model/static: critical windows are enumerated: before write, between writes, before/after commit acknowledgement, before/after outbox/provider callback, receipt/audit finalization and restart.\n- Runtime: deterministic failpoints cover each applicable window; retry/reconcile reaches one legal terminal state without partial invisible effects.\n- Blocks: success-only tests, rollback tested only before the first write, or commit-unknown treated as a generic internal error.\n\n### G31 Target dialect, migration, backup and upgrade recovery\n\n- Applies to the current managed Runtime database cohort and the upgrade paths claimed by the release; code and migrations remain dialect-portable across the supported sqlite/mysql/postgres drivers (see G14), while runtime acceptance evidence is captured only on that cohort's driver.\n- Model/static: compatibility matrix, migration order, lock/statement timeouts, rollback/forward-fix, backup/restore and retention/partition strategy are versioned.\n- Runtime: clean install, previous-version upgrade, interrupted migration, backup restore and Runtime restart pass on the managed cohort with current roles.\n- Blocks: an ephemeral/non-durable database target (for SQLite, `:memory:` cannot prove restart or backup-restore), ignored introspection error, source tests without packaged migration execution, a second profiling engine, or evidence whose dialect differs from the managed cohort. Portability note: no evidence may rest on an engine-proprietary feature as a cross-driver product invariant; multi-dialect runtime evidence is not required for one project cohort.\n\n### G32 Typed evidence, exact denominator and anti-self-report\n\n- Applies to every terminal acceptance claim.\n- Model/static: each case requires typed observation schemas whose validators parse behavior facts, not only files and hashes. Denominator generation is exact-set, resource-closed and capability-specific; evidence producer and identities are bound.\n- Runtime: checker independently verifies HTTP status/route/error, transaction/readback, cursor/query profile, old/new PID and DB identity, worker/provider terminal state, differentiated data-scope isolation and correlation facts.\n- Blocks: arbitrary text satisfying an observation kind, self-authored `passed`, missing/orphan cases, evidence from another cohort, or an incomplete denominator exact-set pass.\n\n## Required release decision\n\n`project verify` and package checks can establish only structural delivery evidence. Terminal `done` requires every applicable G01-G32 family to have current evidence at the level its claim needs. If the current Runtime lacks a required implementation—such as execution-level Report paging, SSE resume, provider reconciliation or enforceable application-layer data scope—the candidate is `acceptance_failed` with a named platform-owner gap. Do not remove the case, lower the claim, or describe the capability as ready.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
