:8,\\\"roles\\\":4,\\\"objects\\\":10,\\\"actions\\\":9,\\\"behaviors\\\":14,\\\"acceptance_scenarios\\\":28},\\\"creates\\\":18,\\\"deletes\\\":0,\\\"diagnostics\\\":[],\\\"files\\\":7,\\\"issue_count\\\":0,\\\"items\\\":18,\\\"sql_files\\\":1,\\\"state\\\":\\\"valid\\\",\\\"unchanged\\\":0,\\\"updates\\\":0}\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-6bd4a3a28483-mysql-model-opt2-01/agent-events.jsonl:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# static source-contract gate: run after writing or changing any handler/domain source and\\n# require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize — same analyzer finalize enforces, so discarded\\n# call results/errors and forbidden raw metadata keys surface locally instead of as a\\n# finalize rejection round\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before the first backend compile or test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse `action context`, `connector definition`, and `connector context` from the canonical command map for exact implementation contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior.\\n\\nImplement approved source gaps by module, then run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize` again before the first backend compile or test; re-finalize whenever Action, Connector, domain, or other composition input changes, and only a current finalization receipt permits backend format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Failure receipts are complete `passed`/`failed`/`skipped_dependency` inventories ([verification.md](references/verification.md)); repair the complete receipt inventory as one batch. After focused tests, pass the semantic canary defined above, then execute one complete Runtime integration round: validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime, route each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner under the recorded mutation scope, and collect the full finding inventory before repair, repeating until it is empty.\\n\\nAfter implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, run the authorization and navigation acceptance before closing this stage: build the delta-scoped Action authorization matrix and the role effective-menu matrix against real Runtime sessions and the persisted database, exactly as specified in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Accept authorization and navigation against real data). Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, or provide credentials — use project-provisioned non-human acceptance identities, or record the exact environment/Foundation blocker. A light batch with no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change omits this acceptance per the template rules. Blueprint declarations or source inspection alone cannot satisfy it.\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-b6f4fe96721f-mysql-implement-opt5-01/agent-events.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n# For a delivered batch, this one command also returns the complete deduplicated\\n# implementation_packet for every Action. Retain it and query it locally; do not\\n# call action context once per Action in the same installed delivery.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# Use the focused command only for a genuinely isolated Action repair or an\\n# audit-bound pending evolution that is outside the installed batch packet.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# static source-contract gate: run after writing or changing any handler/domain source and\\n# require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize — same analyzer finalize enforces, so discarded\\n# call results/errors and forbidden raw metadata keys surface locally instead of as a\\n# finalize rejection round\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before the first backend compile or test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. Its `implementation_packet` is the complete batch coding surface: retain that one result and resolve each Action's document references through the packet's content-addressed `documents[]`. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\nImplement approved source gaps by module, then run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize` again before the first backend compile or test; re-finalize whenever Action, Connector, domain, or other composition input changes, and only a current finalization receipt permits backend format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Failure receipts are complete `passed`/`failed`/`skipped_dependency` inventories ([verification.md](references/verification.md)); repair the complete receipt inventory as one batch. After focused tests, pass the semantic canary defined above, then execute one complete Runtime integration round: validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime, route each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner under the recorded mutation scope, and collect the full finding inventory before repair, repeating until it is empty.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-8b146f6da346-mysql-implement-opt6-02/agent-events.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /private/tmp/domainry-codex-home-m2-opt6-93d20d1b/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n# For a delivered batch, this command returns a bounded envelope and atomically\\n# persists the complete deduplicated implementation_packet. Retain the exact\\n# returned packet path and query only the needed Action/documents locally; do not\\n# call action context once per Action in the same installed delivery.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# Use the focused command only for a genuinely isolated Action repair or an\\n# audit-bound pending evolution that is outside the installed batch packet.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# static source-contract gate: run after writing or changing any handler/domain source and\\n# require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize — same analyzer finalize enforces, so discarded\\n# call results/errors and forbidden raw metadata keys surface locally instead of as a\\n# finalize rejection round\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before the first backend compile or test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. The small result is an envelope: retain its exact `.implementation_packet.path`, identity, and hashes. First read the envelope's `.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}` index. Then query the referenced project-managed packet locally for exactly one `.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)` and resolve only that Action's needed document SHA with `.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content`. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and never load all `actions`, `documents`, or document `content` into Agent context. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-eb0a9cf99f79-mysql-test-opt8-01/agent-events.jsonl:6:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /private/tmp/domainry-codex-home-m2-opt8-2ee614ea/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n# For a delivered batch, this command returns a bounded envelope and atomically\\n# persists the complete deduplicated implementation_packet. Retain the exact\\n# returned packet path and query only the needed Action/documents locally; do not\\n# call action context once per Action in the same installed delivery.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# Use the focused command only for a genuinely isolated Action repair or an\\n# audit-bound pending evolution that is outside the installed batch packet.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source-contract, stale generated Action-test scaffold, and production-package\\n# compile gate: require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize and developer Go test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before developer-run tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. The small result is an envelope: retain its exact `.implementation_packet.path`, identity, and hashes. First read the envelope's `.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}` index. Then query the referenced project-managed packet locally for exactly one `.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)` and resolve only that Action's needed document SHA with `.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content`. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and never load all `actions`, `documents`, or document `content` into Agent context. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\nOne bounded shell pattern is:\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-c1ed2bd40246-mysql-test-opt7-01/agent-events.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /private/tmp/domainry-codex-home-m2-opt7-7230082c/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n# For a delivered batch, this command returns a bounded envelope and atomically\\n# persists the complete deduplicated implementation_packet. Retain the exact\\n# returned packet path and query only the needed Action/documents locally; do not\\n# call action context once per Action in the same installed delivery.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# Use the focused command only for a genuinely isolated Action repair or an\\n# audit-bound pending evolution that is outside the installed batch packet.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source-contract and production-package compile gate: run after writing or changing any\\n# handler/domain source and require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before developer-run tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. The small result is an envelope: retain its exact `.implementation_packet.path`, identity, and hashes. First read the envelope's `.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}` index. Then query the referenced project-managed packet locally for exactly one `.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)` and resolve only that Action's needed document SHA with `.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content`. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and never load all `actions`, `documents`, or document `content` into Agent context. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\nOne bounded shell pattern is:\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m1-crm-opt66-mysql-model-03/agent-model-opt66.jsonl:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,410p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt66/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nFor `requirements_only`, the formal Gate identity is the deterministic requirements-evidence closure exposed as `requirements`: the mandatory non-empty `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md`; root `requirements.md` when it exists; and, when `frontend/src/**` contains a regular source file, the complete regular-file evidence under `frontend/**` after excluding dependency, build, coverage, cache, database, log, backup, and temporary artifacts. Paths and content hashes are sorted and framed, so creation order, timestamps, worktree location, backend files, unrelated root files, and derived frontend files do not affect the identity; symlinks fail closed. A frontend-led or immutable external-reference PRD is valid without root `requirements.md`: the PRD records its authority and immutable frontend snapshot/commit or external citation, and the local frontend closure is bound automatically when present. Do not silently place another mutable local authority elsewhere; make it the canonical root `requirements.md` or incorporate its accepted facts and immutable reference identity in the PRD.\\n\\nDo not guess `--bind source` when no project source exists. After obtaining the Gate ID from `list`, invoke the same Gate runner with `attach --gate-id <gate-id> --status passed --evidence-type source_artifact --evidence-path docs/backend-requirements-prd.md --bind requirements`, or execute an applicable validator once with `run --gate-id <gate-id> --bind requirements -- <validator argv>`. Missing or empty PRD makes the binding unavailable. The requirements-only terminal “all in-scope requirements” Gate uses this closure and does not demand an implementation-coverage receipt; a change to any included evidence makes it stale. Every validation argv must itself fail closed: use no pipeline, or place `set -o pipefail`/`set -euo pipefail` inside the executed shell script before any pipeline; `tee` never turns an internal failure into a passing Gate.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound requirements artifact, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. The implementer may add the minimum contrasting identities needed to prove same-record allow/deny, data-Scope, and requester isolation; these are acceptance fixtures, not evidence of the product's organization size or role cardinality. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only managed project-state root is `.domainry/builder/**`. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate the managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- observable validation, conflict, retry, and replay behavior; discover Runtime-owned stable error categories/codes and choose concurrency/idempotency mechanisms after the business invariant is settled;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, observable validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof; Runtime-owned codes and mechanism details may point to their contract-discovery or implementation-decision row.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nClassify every unresolved choice in the PRD before capability selection. This is the authoritative requirements-only routing rule. An item is a `business_blocker` only when accepted evidence leaves at least two concrete, mutually incompatible choices and choosing among them changes a requirement's explicitly demanded observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow. A missing optional field, metadata value, preference, or mechanism is not a blocker when one deterministic conservative fallback preserves the stated core behavior. Apply the blocker only to the smallest affected requirement/model slice; never block an entire Object, Report, export, or Workflow because one optional field or later preference is open.\\n\\n| Decision class | Boundary | Required handling | Blocks model? |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| `business_blocker` | Two or more concrete, mutually incompatible choices remain, and selecting one changes an explicitly required observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow | Name the incompatible choices and the exact affected requirement/model items. Do not infer the choice from UI, fixtures, labels, legacy behavior, or implementation convenience; obtain a current user decision or accepted requirement evidence. | Yes, only for the smallest affected slice. |\\n| `contract_discovery` | Runtime-owned formal error code/category, canonical identifier or principal/context source, or the mapping of accepted facts to Action, Workflow, metadata, or another published capability | Discover it through the typed client, fixed capability categories, or another formal Runtime contract. If the formal contract is absent, record a Foundation/Runtime owner gap; do not turn it into a product question or invent a representation. | No. It may block affected implementation or acceptance after discovery fails, but not settled business modeling. |\\n| `safe_implementation_decision` | A purely technical, reversible choice that preserves the accepted business invariant, including stable ordering with a unique tie-breaker, pagination maximum, index, internal resource key, and audit, idempotency, or concurrency mechanism | Use the published platform standard; otherwise record one deterministic, conservative safe default with its rationale and evolution path. | No. |\\n| `product_follow_up` | A product preference whose currently documented fallback does not change required observable results, permission, amount meaning, or business state in the accepted scope | Record the assumption, owner, fallback, excluded acceptance scope, and review trigger. Never relabel it `confirmed`. | No. |\\n\\nSecurity and privacy omissions use a conservative `safe_implementation_decision`: least privilege, sensitive by default, and no export by default, with the evolvable assumption recorded. If an explicit requirement demands export or broader disclosure of a sensitive fact, that conflict is a `business_blocker` until its authorization and disclosure boundary is confirmed.\\n\\nUse these nonblocking defaults without inventing product rules:\\n\\n- If exact decimal representation, a comparison threshold, or summation is explicit, absent currency, unit, or numeric range metadata does not block the decimal field, comparison, or aggregation. Keep optional metadata nullable and enforce only stated command preconditions. Block only the smallest comparison/conversion slice when two incompatible units are actually possible and the required result depends on choosing or converting between them.\\n- If an asynchronous approval task and its approved/rejected outcomes are explicit but the host lifecycle has no pending state, put pending state on an independent approval Object and leave the host unchanged until the terminal effect. Missing edit/cancel/resubmit behavior is a `product_follow_up`; use conservative concurrency/idempotency that grants no extra authority and produces no duplicate effect.\\n- If a resource is explicitly non-updatable/non-deletable but no business-role read is required, model the resource and immutable deny rules without inventing a read actor. Prove creation through a formal admin/seed path or persistence evidence; do not fabricate business read permission for acceptance.\\n- If weekday/morning scheduling intent is explicit but timezone or holiday-calendar preference is absent, use the platform/deployment-configured deterministic timezone and a standard weekday cron, then record the preference as `product_follow_up`. Block only when the requirement explicitly demands different multi-timezone or statutory-holiday outcomes and choosing the calendar changes that required result.\\n\\nOnly `business_blocker` blocks model authoring. Unspecified additional metadata, preferences, and reversible mechanisms do not block already settled core behavior. A contract-discovery item or safe implementation decision can still fail its later implementation, security, or acceptance Gate; the classification changes who decides the fact, never the required proof or quality bar.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, settle its observable product invariants and record the applicable technical contracts below. An unresolved business result, permission, amount meaning, or state remains a `business_blocker`; unspecified reversible mechanics use the requirements decision routing above and do not become product questions. Delivery and acceptance must still prove every applicable contract:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size from the platform contract or a recorded safe default of 200, stable ordering with a deterministic unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. Page maximum and tie-breaker are `safe_implementation_decision` items when they do not change an accepted business ordering rule. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured on the current managed Runtime database cohort: SQLite uses `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN`, MySQL uses `EXPLAIN FORMAT=JSON`, and PostgreSQL uses `EXPLAIN (FORMAT JSON)`. Bind the evidence dialect to the managed process receipt's `database_driver`; never provision a second engine only for profiling or fall back to SQLite. Preserve statement/row-count budgets and structured index/full-scan classification. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current requirements/source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. Shell pipelines without an explicit in-script `pipefail` setting are rejected before execution. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound requirements, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-implement.jsonl:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,220p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '221,440p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-43c91fec9277-mysql-implement-control-02/agent-events.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,240p' /Users/tiger/.codex/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# static source-contract gate: run after writing or changing any handler/domain source and\\n# require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize — same analyzer finalize enforces, so discarded\\n# call results/errors and forbidden raw metadata keys surface locally instead of as a\\n# finalize rejection round\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before the first backend compile or test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse `action context`, `connector definition`, and `connector context` from the canonical command map for exact implementation contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior.\\n\\nImplement approved source gaps by module, then run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize` again before the first backend compile or test; re-finalize whenever Action, Connector, domain, or other composition input changes, and only a current finalization receipt permits backend format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Failure receipts are complete `passed`/`failed`/`skipped_dependency` inventories ([verification.md](references/verification.md)); repair the complete receipt inventory as one batch. After focused tests, pass the semantic canary defined above, then execute one complete Runtime integration round: validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime, route each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner under the recorded mutation scope, and collect the full finding inventory before repair, repeating until it is empty.\\n\\nAfter implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, run the authorization and navigation acceptance before closing this stage: build the delta-scoped Action authorization matrix and the role effective-menu matrix against real Runtime sessions and the persisted database, exactly as specified in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Accept authorization and navigation against real data). Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, or provide credentials — use project-provisioned non-human acceptance identities, or record the exact environment/Foundation blocker. A light batch with no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change omits this acceptance per the template rules. Blueprint declarations or source inspection alone cannot satisfy it.\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m2-fieldservice-8b146f6da346-mysql-implement-opt6-03/agent-events.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /private/tmp/domainry-codex-home-m2-opt6-93d20d1b/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or any other Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect its routes, screens, typed API contract, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. Later integration work follows the explicit frontend mutation boundary below: route each mismatch to the owner proved by current contracts and real behavior instead of forcing it into the backend; frontend-owned mismatches become findings, never edits.\\n\\nRead [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) completely before starting a whole-project batch. Use it as the only execution workflow.\\n\\n## Semantic execution invariants\\n\\nBefore model authoring, bind every acceptance journey in the existing PRD/TODO to its requirement, actor, formal Runtime session/principal source, Runtime-derived context and canonical identifiers, isolated setup records, command and read surface, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict oracle, durable effect, and dependent journeys. Do not create a second plan. `project acceptance prepare` must compile these bindings into the denominator's required `domainry-semantic-acceptance-contract-v1`; an unmapped case, actor/role mismatch, unbound command/fact, shared setup, or divergent oracle is a preparation failure, not a harness TODO. Treat Runtime-owned IDs, hierarchy values, principal claims, and other canonical representations as opaque: resolve them through `GET /identity/principal-context`, the published typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime result and carry the returned value unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them; if no formal source exists, keep the affected journey blocked.\\n\\nEvery scope-sensitive journey requires a same-record contrast through real Runtime sessions: the matching actor must observe the targeted non-empty result, a non-matching actor must receive the contractually exact concealment/denial, and a broader actor must observe the record when such a role is part of the requirement. Empty collections, null results, zero aggregates, or an arbitrary HTTP error never prove success unless that exact outcome is the confirmed requirement. Denied/conflict probes also prove unchanged durable state. Use fresh per-scenario identifiers so one case cannot pass from another case's residue.\\n\\nAfter the current source is finalized and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before the broad Runtime integration round. A canary failure blocks dependent journeys: capture the complete root-cause fan-out, repair it once at its owner, and rerun only the invalidated checks. The canary is execution order, not permission to publish a partial model or reduce the frozen acceptance denominator.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Set `DOMAINRY_CLI` to its returned `cli`, which must equal the current package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows), and use its `session_id`, `todo`, compact `resume_packet`, and one-time `authoring_context` for the whole task. The context bundle contains the canonical discovery/schema command sequence, project model inventory, routed reference paths, and small generic decision examples; use it after context compaction instead of repeating broad repository or capability discovery. Re-run the resolver only if the project root or Host session identity changes. This one CLI exposes only the model, capability discovery, Action/Connector implementation, verification, packaging, and Runtime commands listed below. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before any other Builder command. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\"` for every command shown by this Skill and its references; never discover a CLI through `PATH` or substitute another Skill's binary.\\n\\n## Canonical normal-development CLI map\\n\\nUse only the applicable commands below, in TODO order. These are the complete normal project-development command families; do not invent a neighboring command name. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent once selected. Use a command's `--help` for flags when a placeholder needs resolution.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\n# SDK bootstrap when the delivered Runtime Client package or disclosure contract is absent\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# platform capability reuse discovery before modeling\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# backend modeling: edit backend/model/*.json, then inspect the pending plan\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# bootstrap model files once for a project created before the model workflow\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# release: the single release command (full semantics: references/backend-model.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n# existing Builder delivery only: rerun with the CLI-emitted evolution audit SHA-256 and review reason\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps only\\n# For a delivered batch, this command returns a bounded envelope and atomically\\n# persists the complete deduplicated implementation_packet. Retain the exact\\n# returned packet path and query only the needed Action/documents locally; do not\\n# call action context once per Action in the same installed delivery.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# Use the focused command only for a genuinely isolated Action repair or an\\n# audit-bound pending evolution that is outside the installed batch packet.\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# static source-contract gate: run after writing or changing any handler/domain source and\\n# require exit 0 BEFORE every source finalize — same analyzer finalize enforces, so discarded\\n# call results/errors and forbidden raw metadata keys surface locally instead of as a\\n# finalize rejection round\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# re-finalize after implementing approved source gaps, before the first backend compile or test\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification and managed Runtime\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n# freeze the acceptance denominator, run the platform runner first, then check results\\n# (runner-first rule and exact-set contract: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n# after a model evolution only: re-record ONLY the reported stale/missing cases (incremental flow: references/verification.md)\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only release command in this flow; its complete convergence, checklist, destructive-consent (`--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create), and evolution-audit (`evolution_review_required` → rerun with the emitted SHA-256 and review reason) semantics live in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). Always pass the reviewed `--product-surfaces`: it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source, and backend materialization inside apply must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`. After a failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command; never invent a neighboring release command.\\n\\n## Session TODO\\n\\nMaintain exactly one development document for the current Agent session:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nUse the real session/thread ID returned by the context resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; its temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, or reuse another session's ID.\\n\\nAt session start:\\n\\n1. Resolve the current host-provided Agent session ID.\\n2. Resume `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` when the same session already created it.\\n3. Otherwise create that file from [DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md](assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md).\\n4. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, frontend mutation scope, and concrete tasks.\\n5. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order.\\n6. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Bind command receipts or non-empty project-local evidence artifacts to the applicable source, model/Blueprint, and Runtime identities. Before leaving a stage, run its `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`; changed evidence content or bound identity becomes stale and blocks completion.\\n7. At completion, require Gate status for `verify` and `done`, close every finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response; repair any reported inconsistency and rerun until it passes.\\n\\nOne session owns only its own TODO and must not overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even when it continues related project work; it may reference previous session documents as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nDo not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to the current session document, finish the round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate must bind the artifact and state the exact fact it proves; if later work changes the bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, or artifact content, the Gate is stale — rerun or regenerate the evidence. The TODO checker remains only a lightweight consistency linter; the evidence Gate controls step closure, while the verification evidence ladder controls how high the final claim may reach.\\n\\nIf the template's physical order contradicts the real dependency order, fix and validate `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` before using or releasing that Skill version — never compensate by executing items out of order.\\n\\n## Project boundary\\n\\nKeep the project canonical:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  Agent-authored backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  Project-owned Report SQL referenced by model `sql_file`; model apply normalizes, hashes, and embeds it\\nbackend/   project-owned Go source plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session development TODO\\n.domainry/  CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development. Normal editors, file patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source.\\n\\n`frontend/**` remains read-only for requirements/domain extraction in every scope and throughout the entire task under every recorded `Frontend mutation scope`: this Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes a `frontend/**` path — the scope records the authorization context only.\\n\\n`.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination-file exception under `.domainry/`; every file is owned by the matching Agent session. Every other `.domainry/` path keeps its existing CLI/platform ownership.\\n\\nNever hand-edit:\\n\\n- generated source;\\n- frozen Project Contracts;\\n- Runtime manifests;\\n- materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts;\\n- audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state.\\n\\nDo not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\n### Frontend mutation boundary\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requests a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the work to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms `frontend/**` is project-owned.\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limits the task to backend work.\\n- `externally_owned`: the frontend is delivered by an external tool or owner that this task must not write.\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records the authorization boundary only; it never changes what this Skill edits. During integration, compare the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response before assigning a defect. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a frontend compatibility adapter, fabricated fallback, or altered Mock. When evidence assigns a defect to `frontend/**` or a Mock, record it as a finding — owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, contract basis — in every scope, including `integration_authorized`; never edit `frontend/**`. A frontend-owned defect that truly blocks a backend journey makes that journey explicitly blocked; do not mislabel the defect as a platform owner gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance in this Skill is backend end-to-end: real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior, bound to the same TODO and evidence Gate source identity ([verification.md](references/verification.md)). Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\nIdentity binding through the context resolver and `version --json` is setup, not a workflow stage or TODO gate (see Loaded Skill and CLI identity above). The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, or migration command surface.\\n\\nEvery V2 project command uses `.domainry/builder/**` as its only managed Builder state root; legacy-root migration semantics are in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Initialization) — never move, merge, or recreate a managed root by hand.\\n\\nFor a new project, verify the project root itself is one initialized Git worktree before any project-mutating CLI command (exact procedure in the guide); never initialize an existing project without authorization or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\nBefore metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. The small result is an envelope: retain its exact `.implementation_packet.path`, identity, and hashes. First read the envelope's `.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}` index. Then query the referenced project-managed packet locally for exactly one `.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)` and resolve only that Action's needed document SHA with `.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content`. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and never load all `actions`, `documents`, or document `content` into Agent context. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\nOne bounded shell pattern is:\\n\\n```sh\\nSOURCE_PREPARE_JSON=$(\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\")\\nprintf '%s\\\\n' \\\"$SOURCE_PREPARE_JSON\\\" | jq '.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}'\\nPACKET_RELATIVE=$(printf '%s\\\\n' \\\"$SOURCE_PREPARE_JSON\\\" | jq -er '.implementation_packet.path')\\nPACKET_PATH=\\\"$PROJECT_ROOT/$PACKET_RELATIVE\\\"\\njq --arg action_key \\\"$ACTION_KEY\\\" '.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)' \\\"$PACKET_PATH\\\"\\njq -r --arg sha256 \\\"$DOCUMENT_SHA256\\\" '.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content' \\\"$PACKET_PATH\\\"\\n```\\n\\nPreserve `PACKET_RELATIVE` exactly as returned; do not substitute `latest.json`, search by packet hash, copy the packet, or reconstruct its managed path.\\n\\nImplement approved source gaps by module, then run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize` again before the first backend compile or test; re-finalize whenever Action, Connector, domain, or other composition input changes, and only a current finalization receipt permits backend format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Failure receipts are complete `passed`/`failed`/`skipped_dependency` inventories ([verification.md](references/verification.md)); repair the complete receipt inventory as one batch. After focused tests, pass the semantic canary defined above, then execute one complete Runtime integration round: validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime, route each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner under the recorded mutation scope, and collect the full finding inventory before repair, repeating until it is empty.\\n\\nAfter implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, run the authorization and navigation acceptance before closing this stage: build the delta-scoped Action authorization matrix and the role effective-menu matrix against real Runtime sessions and the persisted database, exactly as specified in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) (Accept authorization and navigation against real data). Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, or provide credentials — use project-provisioned non-human acceptance identities, or record the exact environment/Foundation blocker. A light batch with no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change omits this acceptance per the template rules. Blueprint declarations or source inspection alone cannot satisfy it.\\n\\n## verify\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](references/evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](references/verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md), then run the final verification. Apply the complete G01-G32 denominator derived before modeling; user examples never reduce it. `project verify` and packaging establish only L1 `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, never L3 `scenario_verified` real-data behavior; project verification, packaging, and Runtime launch are internal checks inside this stage, not additional stages or start locks. Run the complete check-and-evidence inventory specified in [verification.md](references/verification.md).\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt44-mysql-generality-01/agent-apply-retry.jsonl:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '1,900p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     390 /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\n# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permissio\n... 346789 bytes omitted ...\ne metadata modeling, inspect the delivered frontend read-only and convert it into a backend dependency contract. For a new project or a complete Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from [BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md](assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md). For a light existing-project increment, record a traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the existing backend PRD; do not manufacture a second full document when the accepted baseline remains valid.\\n\\nWhen the input requirement document is already structured, author the PRD in **reference mode** (rules in the guide): cite source sections instead of restating them and write only supplements — a supplement layer typically under one third of the source size, with the critical-ambiguity table and traceability unrelaxed.\\n\\nRecord `Execution scope: requirements_only` when the user asks only for analysis, requirements, or domain design and says not to develop. In that scope, stop after the requirements and domain-truth Gates, omit model/apply/implementation/Runtime/verify sections, and close `done` only as a requirements artifact result; do not claim delivery, integration, or any L1-L5 implementation evidence. Every build, repair, integration, or acceptance request uses `Execution scope: implementation` and continues through the full applicable workflow.\\n\\nUse the evidence precedence order and the `confirmed`/`recommended`/`open`/`frontend_only`/`legacy_reference` classification defined in [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md); preserve conflicts instead of silently selecting the most convenient source, and keep concrete file/route evidence.\\n\\nExtract the complete requirement inventory (routes/journeys, actors/roles, objects/fields, queries, commands, states, calculations, authorization/scope, errors, transactions/idempotency, schedules/connectors, external effects, frontend-only presentation — full checklist in the guide) and build the trace `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner → metadata/source implementation → permission/scope → acceptance journey`. A visual affordance is evidence of intent, not proof of its business rule.\\n\\nDo not infer critical rules: an unresolved critical ambiguity (the guide's Resolve critical ambiguity list) blocks domain-truth design and all affected downstream modeling. Non-critical open items may proceed only when their assumption, owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope are explicit.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\nBefore materializing the SDK, discovering capabilities, or authoring metadata, read [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](references/domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely and close the domain-truth Gate: distinguish occurred facts, summaries, and simulations, and close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. Historical financial facts must never read current Settings to recompute.\\n\\nWhen a business event locks money, commission, points, inventory cost, or payroll input, require an atomic source-owned settlement Action with immutable snapshots and ledger entries per that document; corrections append, never mutate. Do not proceed to capability discovery while any affected ownership, persistence, or correction decision is open.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nDo not discover or select a Report capability until all three contracts close:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every Report returning rows has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` + opaque `cursor`, maximum page size, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, and explicit `truncated`/`total` semantics. Unbounded results, fetch-all, and browser-memory pagination are prohibited.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** prepare executes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted probe of `0..1000` rows yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 routes immediately to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; a count-only or export-only second query is prohibited.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** the `>1000` worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares the canonical authorization Scope before page one, failing closed on real drift; it never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through the export job to audit and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version; equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact (no second file, download record, or terminal notification), `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint, and only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create. Full replay semantics: [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md).\\n\\nTreat each line as a blocking design and acceptance Gate, not optional guidance. A published route or paginated response shape does not prove execution-level boundedness: require `pagination_trace` and `query_profile` evidence (captured on the SQLite acceptance database via `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` — never provision another database engine for profiling) and a signed cursor strategy per [reports.md](references/capabilities/reports.md); read that document whenever a page or API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the active delivery cannot produce that proof, Report list/export scope is `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap — do not invent project infrastructure, exclude an in-scope requirement, or claim support.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](references/production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive the current model's G01-G32 applicability matrix. This is an early prevention Gate: transaction, exact-value, authorization/RLS, paging/index, timeout/capacity, async/provider, SSE/Agent, observability, recovery, migration and evidence requirements must shape the model before publication instead of appearing for the first time in final acceptance.\\n\\n## model\\n\\nDerive one global resource-demand pool from the accepted backend dependency PRD and its frontend evidence. Reconcile canonical Objects, fields, Relations, Actions, Roles, permissions, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, Agent Skills/Agents/Tasks/Entrypoints/Service Principals, Identity menu entries, and role-menu assignments. Assign every canonical resource one owner module and resolve conflicts before authoring the backend model files. Before the first publication, also model the new-project acceptance prerequisites — non-human acceptance identities, Workforce/departments, minimum journey seed records (see the guide) — never deferring them to final verify or seeding online credentials. Business menu definitions and role-menu assignments are not Blueprint model collections: plan them as a post-apply configuration step through the foundation Identity menu boundary (see [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md)).\\n\\nFor an existing project, first understand current requirements, source, interfaces, metadata, tests, and delivery state. Plan the requested increment and its regression closure without rewriting valid existing work.\\n\\nAfter the domain-truth Gate closes, use existing metadata capabilities first:\\n\\n- Runtime CRUD and standard record operations;\\n- state machine and Workflow;\\n- Scheduler and Automation;\\n- record views and Reports;\\n- Agent Skill/Agent/Task/Entrypoint metadata and Workflow `agent_task` nodes when model reasoning is required;\\n- published Connector operations.\\n\\nCreate project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express.\\n\\nBefore capability discovery, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK exists. If `.domainry/builder/runtime-client-sdk/packages/runtime-client` or its capability disclosure is absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the same pinned target used for the batch. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fabricate or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nBefore declaring a capability gap, check whether the required operation already exists as published platform capability: load the fixed category index once, then each selected category exactly once, using the two `contract capability-*` commands from the canonical command map. Choose only keys returned by the index; map every required operation to a returned endpoint or record the exact owner gap. An `operations_only` result proves the capability exists but is isolated from Business Workspace — it is not a missing backend capability. Admin frontend location is not backend ownership or authorization; never use `contract search` to discover Admin capability. Full discovery and reconciliation rules: [runtime-client.md](references/runtime-client.md).\\n\\nBefore authoring metadata, read [capabilities/README.md](references/capabilities/README.md), select the matching task profile, and load only its routed business capability documents. Read [ownership-and-extensions.md](references/ownership-and-extensions.md), [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md), and [authorization.md](references/authorization.md) when their subject is in scope.\\n\\nUse the `project source prepare` implementation packet for installed-batch Action contracts; use focused `action context` only for the isolated cases named in the canonical command map. Use `connector definition` and `connector context` for Connector contracts. V2 exposes no generic contract search command.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\nAuthor the complete backend metadata as declarative model files under `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; read [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md) for the exact file contract. Declare every module's complete inventory (Objects, Relations, Actions, Roles, authorization, data scopes, Workflows, state machines, Reports, Schedulers, Automations, Connectors, menus, seeds, acceptance) in the files before the first `model validate`: partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Before that first validation, close the cross-resource preflight and its strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` field validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Then repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating instead of probing one resource family at a time. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft: do not add speculative resources or enrich identity/bootstrap metadata while recording evidence. A requirement-backed model change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again. Use `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan` to inspect the pending create/update/delete/unchanged classification against the current Ledger without writing anything, and never use `apply` as a schema probe. For an existing project created before this workflow, bootstrap once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export`.\\n\\nRelease once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces>`, adding one pinned explicit `--environment` or `--service` target. After a stage failure the fix is repairing the model files and re-running the same command; the full convergence, checklist, destructive-consent, and evolution-audit semantics are in [backend-model.md](references/backend-model.md). The surface value is required up front so a backend-only batch still binds its Runtime/SDK integration target before publication; it does not authorize frontend page mutation.\\n\\nRun `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once after materialization and before implementing approved Action gaps. It creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons and never overwrites existing project source; the skeletons fail closed with `*.not_implemented`, so they are an implementation inventory, not completed business behavior. The small result is an envelope: retain its exact `.implementation_packet.path`, identity, and hashes. First read the envelope's `.actions[] | {action_key,source_path,source_state,test_path,test_state}` index. Then query the referenced project-managed packet locally for exactly one `.actions[] | select(.action_key == $action_key)` and resolve only that Action's needed document SHA with `.documents[] | select(.sha256 == $sha256) | .content`. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and never load all `actions`, `documents`, or document `content` into Agent context. Do not rediscover the same installed delivery through repeated `action context` calls or generated-source reads. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution not represented by that packet.\\n\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m1-crm-opt65-mysql-generality-01/agent-requirements-opt65-reclassify.jsonl:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nFor `requirements_only`, the formal Gate identity is the deterministic requirements-evidence closure exposed as `requirements`: the mandatory non-empty `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md`; root `requirements.md` when it exists; and, when `frontend/src/**` contains a regular source file, the complete regular-file evidence under `frontend/**` after excluding dependency, build, coverage, cache, database, log, backup, and temporary artifacts. Paths and content hashes are sorted and framed, so creation order, timestamps, worktree location, backend files, unrelated root files, and derived frontend files do not affect the identity; symlinks fail closed. A frontend-led or immutable external-reference PRD is valid without root `requirements.md`: the PRD records its authority and immutable frontend snapshot/commit or external citation, and the local frontend closure is bound automatically when present. Do not silently place another mutable local authority elsewhere; make it the canonical root `requirements.md` or incorporate its accepted facts and immutable reference identity in the PRD.\\n\\nDo not guess `--bind source` when no project source exists. After obtaining the Gate ID from `list`, invoke the same Gate runner with `attach --gate-id <gate-id> --status passed --evidence-type source_artifact --evidence-path docs/backend-requirements-prd.md --bind requirements`, or execute an applicable validator once with `run --gate-id <gate-id> --bind requirements -- <validator argv>`. Missing or empty PRD makes the binding unavailable. The requirements-only terminal “all in-scope requirements” Gate uses this closure and does not demand an implementation-coverage receipt; a change to any included evidence makes it stale. Every validation argv must itself fail closed: use no pipeline, or place `set -o pipefail`/`set -euo pipefail` inside the executed shell script before any pipeline; `tee` never turns an internal failure into a passing Gate.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound requirements artifact, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. The implementer may add the minimum contrasting identities needed to prove same-record allow/deny, data-Scope, and requester isolation; these are acceptance fixtures, not evidence of the product's organization size or role cardinality. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only managed project-state root is `.domainry/builder/**`. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate the managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- observable validation, conflict, retry, and replay behavior; discover Runtime-owned stable error categories/codes and choose concurrency/idempotency mechanisms after the business invariant is settled;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, observable validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof; Runtime-owned codes and mechanism details may point to their contract-discovery or implementation-decision row.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nClassify every unresolved choice in the PRD before capability selection. This is the authoritative requirements-only routing rule. An item is a `business_blocker` only when accepted evidence leaves at least two concrete, mutually incompatible choices and choosing among them changes a requirement's explicitly demanded observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow. A missing optional field, metadata value, preference, or mechanism is not a blocker when one deterministic conservative fallback preserves the stated core behavior. Apply the blocker only to the smallest affected requirement/model slice; never block an entire Object, Report, export, or Workflow because one optional field or later preference is open.\\n\\n| Decision class | Boundary | Required handling | Blocks model? |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| `business_blocker` | Two or more concrete, mutually incompatible choices remain, and selecting one changes an explicitly required observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow | Name the incompatible choices and the exact affected requirement/model items. Do not infer the choice from UI, fixtures, labels, legacy behavior, or implementation convenience; obtain a current user decision or accepted requirement evidence. | Yes, only for the smallest affected slice. |\\n| `contract_discovery` | Runtime-owned formal error code/category, canonical identifier or principal/context source, or the mapping of accepted facts to Action, Workflow, metadata, or another published capability | Discover it through the typed client, fixed capability categories, or another formal Runtime contract. If the formal contract is absent, record a Foundation/Runtime owner gap; do not turn it into a product question or invent a representation. | No. It may block affected implementation or acceptance after discovery fails, but not settled business modeling. |\\n| `safe_implementation_decision` | A purely technical, reversible choice that preserves the accepted business invariant, including stable ordering with a unique tie-breaker, pagination maximum, index, internal resource key, and audit, idempotency, or concurrency mechanism | Use the published platform standard; otherwise record one deterministic, conservative safe default with its rationale and evolution path. | No. |\\n| `product_follow_up` | A product preference whose currently documented fallback does not change required observable results, permission, amount meaning, or business state in the accepted scope | Record the assumption, owner, fallback, excluded acceptance scope, and review trigger. Never relabel it `confirmed`. | No. |\\n\\nSecurity and privacy omissions use a conservative `safe_implementation_decision`: least privilege, sensitive by default, and no export by default, with the evolvable assumption recorded. If an explicit requirement demands export or broader disclosure of a sensitive fact, that conflict is a `business_blocker` until its authorization and disclosure boundary is confirmed.\\n\\nUse these nonblocking defaults without inventing product rules:\\n\\n- If exact decimal representation, a comparison threshold, or summation is explicit, absent currency, unit, or numeric range metadata does not block the decimal field, comparison, or aggregation. Keep optional metadata nullable and enforce only stated command preconditions. Block only the smallest comparison/conversion slice when two incompatible units are actually possible and the required result depends on choosing or converting between them.\\n- If an asynchronous approval task and its approved/rejected outcomes are explicit but the host lifecycle has no pending state, put pending state on an independent approval Object and leave the host unchanged until the terminal effect. Missing edit/cancel/resubmit behavior is a `product_follow_up`; use conservative concurrency/idempotency that grants no extra authority and produces no duplicate effect.\\n- If a resource is explicitly non-updatable/non-deletable but no business-role read is required, model the resource and immutable deny rules without inventing a read actor. Prove creation through a formal admin/seed path or persistence evidence; do not fabricate business read permission for acceptance.\\n- If weekday/morning scheduling intent is explicit but timezone or holiday-calendar preference is absent, use the platform/deployment-configured deterministic timezone and a standard weekday cron, then record the preference as `product_follow_up`. Block only when the requirement explicitly demands different multi-timezone or statutory-holiday outcomes and choosing the calendar changes that required result.\\n\\nOnly `business_blocker` blocks model authoring. Unspecified additional metadata, preferences, and reversible mechanisms do not block already settled core behavior. A contract-discovery item or safe implementation decision can still fail its later implementation, security, or acceptance Gate; the classification changes who decides the fact, never the required proof or quality bar.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, settle its observable product invariants and record the applicable technical contracts below. An unresolved business result, permission, amount meaning, or state remains a `business_blocker`; unspecified reversible mechanics use the requirements decision routing above and do not become product questions. Delivery and acceptance must still prove every applicable contract:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size from the platform contract or a recorded safe default of 200, stable ordering with a deterministic unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. Page maximum and tie-breaker are `safe_implementation_decision` items when they do not change an accepted business ordering rule. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current requirements/source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. Shell pipelines without an explicit in-script `pipefail` setting are rejected before execution. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound requirements, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/m1-crm-opt65-mysql-generality-01/agent-requirements-opt65-reclassify.jsonl:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '1,140p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '141,280p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '281,440p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     410 /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt65/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\n# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nFor `requirements_only`, the formal Gate identity is the deterministic requirements-evidence closure exposed as `requirements`: the mandatory non-empty `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md`; root `requirements.md` when it exists; and, when `frontend/src/**` contains a regular source file, the complete regular-file evidence under `frontend/**` after excluding dependency, build, coverage, cache, database, log, backup, and temporary artifacts. Paths and content hashes are sorted and framed, so creation order, timestamps, worktree location, backend files, unrelated root files, and derived frontend files do not affect the identity; symlinks fail closed. A frontend-led or immutable external-reference PRD is valid without root `requirements.md`: the PRD records its authority and immutable frontend snapshot/commit or external citation, and the local frontend closure is bound automatically when present. Do not silently place another mutable local authority elsewhere; make it the canonical root `requirements.md` or incorporate its accepted facts and immutable reference identity in the PRD.\\n\\nDo not guess `--bind source` when no project source exists. After obtaining the Gate ID from `list`, invoke the same Gate runner with `attach --gate-id <gate-id> --status passed --evidence-type source_artifact --evidence-path docs/backend-requirements-prd.md --bind requirements`, or execute an applicable validator once with `run --gate-id <gate-id> --bind requirements -- <validator argv>`. Missing or empty PRD makes the binding unavailable. The requirements-only terminal “all in-scope requirements” Gate uses this closure and does not demand an implementation-coverage receipt; a change to any included evidence makes it stale. Every validation argv must itself fail closed: use no pipeline, or place `set -o pipefail`/`set -euo pipefail` inside the executed shell script before any pipeline; `tee` never turns an internal failure into a passing Gate.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound requirements artifact, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. The implementer may add the minimum contrasting identities needed to prove same-record allow/deny, data-Scope, and requester isolation; these are acceptance fixtures, not evidence of the product's organization size or role cardinality. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only managed project-state root is `.domainry/builder/**`. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate the managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- observable validation, conflict, retry, and replay behavior; discover Runtime-owned stable error categories/codes and choose concurrency/idempotency mechanisms after the business invariant is settled;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, observable validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof; Runtime-owned codes and mechanism details may point to their contract-discovery or implementation-decision row.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nClassify every unresolved choice in the PRD before capability selection. This is the authoritative requirements-only routing rule. An item is a `business_blocker` only when accepted evidence leaves at least two concrete, mutually incompatible choices and choosing among them changes a requirement's explicitly demanded observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow. A missing optional field, metadata value, preference, or mechanism is not a blocker when one deterministic conservative fallback preserves the stated core behavior. Apply the blocker only to the smallest affected requirement/model slice; never block an entire Object, Report, export, or Workflow because one optional field or later preference is open.\\n\\n| Decision class | Boundary | Required handling | Blocks model? |\\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\\n| `business_blocker` | Two or more concrete, mutually incompatible choices remain, and selecting one changes an explicitly required observable business result, authorization allow/deny result, amount/unit semantics, irreversible external effect, or business-state flow | Name the incompatible choices and the exact affected requirement/model items. Do not infer the choice from UI, fixtures, labels, legacy behavior, or implementation convenience; obtain a current user decision or accepted requirement evidence. | Yes, only for the smallest affected slice. |\\n| `contract_discovery` | Runtime-owned formal error code/category, canonical identifier or principal/context source, or the mapping of accepted facts to Action, Workflow, metadata, or another published capability | Discover it through the typed client, fixed capability categories, or another formal Runtime contract. If the formal contract is absent, record a Foundation/Runtime owner gap; do not turn it into a product question or invent a representation. | No. It may block affected implementation or acceptance after discovery fails, but not settled business modeling. |\\n| `safe_implementation_decision` | A purely technical, reversible choice that preserves the accepted business invariant, including stable ordering with a unique tie-breaker, pagination maximum, index, internal resource key, and audit, idempotency, or concurrency mechanism | Use the published platform standard; otherwise record one deterministic, conservative safe default with its rationale and evolution path. | No. |\\n| `product_follow_up` | A product preference whose currently documented fallback does not change required observable results, permission, amount meaning, or business state in the accepted scope | Record the assumption, owner, fallback, excluded acceptance scope, and review trigger. Never relabel it `confirmed`. | No. |\\n\\nSecurity and privacy omissions use a conservative `safe_implementation_decision`: least privilege, sensitive by default, and no export by default, with the evolvable assumption recorded. If an explicit requirement demands export or broader disclosure of a sensitive fact, that conflict is a `business_blocker` until its authorization and disclosure boundary is confirmed.\\n\\nUse these nonblocking defaults without inventing product rules:\\n\\n- If exact decimal representation, a comparison threshold, or summation is explicit, absent currency, unit, or numeric range metadata does not block the decimal field, comparison, or aggregation. Keep optional metadata nullable and enforce only stated command preconditions. Block only the smallest comparison/conversion slice when two incompatible units are actually possible and the required result depends on choosing or converting between them.\\n- If an asynchronous approval task and its approved/rejected outcomes are explicit but the host lifecycle has no pending state, put pending state on an independent approval Object and leave the host unchanged until the terminal effect. Missing edit/cancel/resubmit behavior is a `product_follow_up`; use conservative concurrency/idempotency that grants no extra authority and produces no duplicate effect.\\n- If a resource is explicitly non-updatable/non-deletable but no business-role read is required, model the resource and immutable deny rules without inventing a read actor. Prove creation through a formal admin/seed path or persistence evidence; do not fabricate business read permission for acceptance.\\n- If weekday/morning scheduling intent is explicit but timezone or holiday-calendar preference is absent, use the platform/deployment-configured deterministic timezone and a standard weekday cron, then record the preference as `product_follow_up`. Block only when the requirement explicitly demands different multi-timezone or statutory-holiday outcomes and choosing the calendar changes that required result.\\n\\nOnly `business_blocker` blocks model authoring. Unspecified additional metadata, preferences, and reversible mechanisms do not block already settled core behavior. A contract-discovery item or safe implementation decision can still fail its later implementation, security, or acceptance Gate; the classification changes who decides the fact, never the required proof or quality bar.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, settle its observable product invariants and record the applicable technical contracts below. An unresolved business result, permission, amount meaning, or state remains a `business_blocker`; unspecified reversible mechanics use the requirements decision routing above and do not become product questions. Delivery and acceptance must still prove every applicable contract:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size from the platform contract or a recorded safe default of 200, stable ordering with a deterministic unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. Page maximum and tie-breaker are `safe_implementation_decision` items when they do not change an accepted business ordering rule. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current requirements/source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. Shell pipelines without an explicit in-script `pipefail` setting are rejected before execution. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound requirements, source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-runtime-start.jsonl:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,430p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt39/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-semantic-canary-opt43-retry.jsonl:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt43/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '141,280p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt43/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-source-repair-opt44-cache-retry.jsonl:5:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_1\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/SKILL.md\\nsed -n '1,140p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '141,280p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '281,390p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"---\\nname: domainry-builder-v1\\ndescription: Extract an evidence-backed backend dependency PRD from a delivered frontend, then build, evolve, repair, integrate, and verify the matching Domainry source-owned application through one project-visible development TODO. The Skill keeps frontend evidence read-only while settling requirements and domain truth, authors declarative backend/model/*.json metadata, and records evidence-located frontend defects as findings for the frontend owner instead of repairing them. Use for frontend-to-Runtime replacement, new backend projects, existing-project increments, metadata-first backend implementation, Runtime integration, full-stack defect routing, and final verification.\\n---\\n\\n# Domainry Builder V1\\n\\nDrive every development batch through:\\n\\n```text\\nrequirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done\\n```\\n\\nPlatform capabilities determine the implementation mapping; they never determine the business facts. Do not start from Dataset, Report, metadata, an Action shape, or another Runtime feature and work backward into a domain model. Settle occurred facts, summaries, simulations, calculation ownership, persistence, and correction before capability discovery. An unresolved critical business fact blocks affected modeling; implementation convenience is not evidence.\\n\\nThe frontend under `frontend/**` is evidence of product demand, not automatically a complete business specification. Inspect routes, screens, typed API contracts, Mock adapters, tests, roles, states, and copy read-only while settling requirements and domain truth; never promote Mock behavior into business truth. This Skill never creates, modifies, or deletes `frontend/**`. Route each mismatch to the owner proved by the explicit requirement, published Runtime contract, typed frontend API, Mock/tests, and real behavior. A frontend- or Mock-owned mismatch is a finding for owner `frontend`, never a backend workaround or frontend edit.\\n\\n## Route exactly once\\n\\nChoose one route before loading supporting resources. Do not combine the routes.\\n\\n### Whole-project development\\n\\nFor every normal requirements, build, repair, integration, acceptance, or whole-project continuation batch, read [development-guide.md](references/development-guide.md) **completely before starting** and use it as the only execution workflow. It contains the authoritative TODO/Gate process, full normal-development CLI map, project boundaries, phase rules, required reference routing, Runtime acceptance, and terminal policy. Follow every applicable routed reference it requires. A full batch reads the whole guide; a light batch may omit only TODO sections under the guide's explicit light-batch rules, never guide loading or an applicable authority.\\n\\nThe routing map below is a reachability contract, not permission to read only selected guide sections:\\n\\n| Moved entrypoint section / contract | Whole-project authority |\\n| --- | --- |\\n| workflow, routing, and batch scale | [whole-project workflow](references/development-guide.md#whole-project-workflow) |\\n| loaded Skill, CLI identity, and normal command surface | [CLI identity and canonical command map](references/development-guide.md#cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map) |\\n| complete TODO, Gate first-execution receipts, and session ownership | [session TODO and Gate receipts](references/development-guide.md#session-todo-and-gate-receipts) |\\n| project ownership and frontend finding routing | [project and frontend boundaries](references/development-guide.md#project-and-frontend-boundaries) |\\n| requirements extraction and critical ambiguity | [requirements to domain facts](references/development-guide.md#requirements-to-domain-facts) |\\n| domain truth, settlement, list Report, and technical prevention Gates | [domain truth design](references/development-guide.md#domain-truth-design) |\\n| capability reconciliation and metadata modeling | [model and capability mapping](references/development-guide.md#model-and-capability-mapping) |\\n| declarative authoring and the only release command | [model apply is the only release](references/development-guide.md#model-apply-is-the-only-release) |\\n| bounded installed-batch Action context | [source prepare implementation packet](references/development-guide.md#source-prepare-implementation-packet) |\\n| valid check, source finalize, focused tests, and exact receipts | [project check to finalize to tests](references/development-guide.md#project-check-to-finalize-to-tests) |\\n| semantic journey binding and highest-risk canary | [semantic execution and canary](references/development-guide.md#semantic-execution-and-canary) |\\n| managed Runtime and exact-denominator acceptance | [Runtime and acceptance](references/development-guide.md#runtime-and-acceptance) |\\n| evidence ceiling and terminal states | [done terminal policy](references/development-guide.md#done-terminal-policy) |\\n| initial credential and acceptance-identity safety | [system seed login](references/development-guide.md#system-seed-login) |\\n\\n### Explicit source-finalization continuation\\n\\nUse this route only when the caller explicitly requests `source-finalization-continuation`, accepts and freezes upstream requirements/modeling, confirms the current source-finalization checkpoint remains valid, and limits scope exactly to:\\n\\n```text\\nvalid Actions project check → source finalize → module-focused Go test\\n```\\n\\nRun the loaded Skill's resolver once with an explicit project-relative focused package:\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" \\\\\\n  --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" \\\\\\n  --profile source-finalization-continuation \\\\\\n  --focused-test-package <project-relative-./package>\\n```\\n\\nThe returned `continuation` envelope atomically owns this session's three-Gate TODO, synchronized Gate IDs, current checkpoint identity, and exact `run_recipes`. Execute those recipes in order and use no other workflow. Do **not** read `references/development-guide.md`, `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`, or any whole-project reference for this profile.\\n\\nThis route is caller authorization, never inferred from project state. Missing, stale, unsafe, or ambiguous session/checkpoint/CLI identity, a package outside the project, a missing focused package, or a conflicting/partial session TODO fails closed. Do not repair such a conflict by deleting or rewriting managed state. The compact route does not bypass the Host's complete loading of this `SKILL.md` and does not change whole-project behavior.\\n\\n## Loaded Skill and CLI identity\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact filesystem path of the `SKILL.md` loaded for this task. Every Builder command, resolver, Gate runner, template, and reference must come from that same root. The package has one executable: `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Never discover it through `PATH`, substitute another installed Skill's binary, call Domainry Plane directly, or invent a neighboring command.\\n\\nFor whole-project work, the guide requires the default resolver once at task start and binds `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Host session ID, TODO, compact resume packet, one-time authoring context, and package identity to its result. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. For the explicit continuation, the compact resolver call above supplies and validates the same package CLI identity without loading whole-project context.\\n\\n## Universal safety and evidence invariants\\n\\n- Treat Runtime-owned identifiers, hierarchy values, principal claims, cursors, and other canonical representations as opaque. Resolve them through the published typed client, `GET /identity/principal-context`, a formal seed/admin path, or a persisted Runtime result and carry them unchanged. Never construct, trim, pad, normalize, inject, or query Runtime-owned persistence to discover them. A missing formal source blocks the affected journey.\\n- Keep project-owned authoring in `backend/model/*.json`, referenced `backend/reports/*.sql`, project-owned backend source/tests, and the current session's `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`. Never hand-edit generated source, frozen contracts, Runtime manifests, receipts, audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned `.domainry/**` delivery state. Never create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that ignores its generated capabilities.\\n- `model apply` is the only normal-development release command. Its reviewed `--product-surfaces` value is an integration boundary, never frontend mutation authorization. Repair reported model/input failures and rerun the same command; never split publication/materialization or bypass destructive/evolution review.\\n- Every check, finalize, and focused-test command must run through its own Gate on its first real execution. Never run it directly first, rerun merely to obtain evidence, search historical receipts, or attach another command's receipt. A receipt proves only its original Gate and current bound identities; changed bound source/model/Runtime/artifact content makes it stale.\\n- Before model authoring, bind every acceptance journey to its requirement, formal actor/session/principal, canonical context and identifiers, isolated setup, command/read surfaces, non-empty positive oracle, exact denied/conflict and unchanged-state oracle, durable effect, and dependencies. Do not create a second plan. Scope-sensitive journeys use same-record contrasts through real Runtime sessions; arbitrary errors, empty results, and residue from another case do not prove success. For unchanged/replay record hashes, use `runtimeRecordDurableSnapshot` (or copied-harness `durable_record_snapshot`), never the whole HTTP response body.\\n- After current source finalization and focused tests pass, run one highest-risk semantic canary across formal identity/context resolution → command → persisted effect → actor-visible read-back before broad Runtime integration. A canary failure blocks dependents and is repaired once at its owner; it never shrinks the frozen model or acceptance denominator.\\n- Never claim above current evidence. Structural verification/package success cannot establish real-data scenario acceptance. Completion requires the applicable evidence Gate, an empty finding inventory, the guide's terminal policy, and the TODO consistency check; unresolved product prerequisites are implementation/Foundation defects, not cosmetic limitations.\\n# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-semantic-canary-opt41-retry.jsonl:8:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_3\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,260p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '261,520p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '521,780p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '781,1040p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The committed CLI response and therefore the default/recipe expectation is `domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1`; the separately advertised `project.check` capability `domainry-project-check-v2` does not rewrite that response contract. The runner accepts only the explicitly schema-validated response contracts `domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1`, `domainry-project-check-v1`, and `domainry-project-check-v2`; unknown contracts, non-JSON, oversized output, and invalid rows remain hash-only. For a supported structured result, even a failed expectation retains a bounded field-allowlisted diagnostic proof (`code`, redacted `message`, project-relative `path`, location, symbol, action, event, variable, owner, and category) so the complete repair batch can be read without persisting arbitrary command output. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Keep verification and packaging as two independent development Gates and execute their exact `project verify --json` and `project package --json` commands separately; neither command receipt can close the other Gate. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-semantic-canary-opt41-retry.jsonl:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_4\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '1,140p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '141,280p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     390 /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt41/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\n# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-source-repair-opt44.jsonl:7:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_2\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"sed -n '1,140p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\nsed -n '141,280p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/codex-home-opt44/skills/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-model.jsonl:10:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_5\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md'\",\"aggregated_output\":\"# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n/Users/tiger/Projects/domainry-skill-evals/runs/s1-ticketing-opt36-mysql-generality-01-legacy/agent-model.jsonl:12:{\"type\":\"item.completed\",\"item\":{\"id\":\"item_6\",\"type\":\"command_execution\",\"command\":\"/bin/zsh -lc \\\"wc -l /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '1,180p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md && sed -n '181,390p' /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\\"\",\"aggregated_output\":\"     390 /Users/tiger/.codex-run/domainry-plane-opt31c-262ca059-8283/skills-opt36/domainry-builder-v1/references/development-guide.md\\n# Project development guide\\n\\n<a id=\\\"whole-project-workflow\\\"></a>\\n\\nDrive every development batch with one visible project document:\\n\\n```text\\n<project>/.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md\\n```\\n\\nThe complete frontend under `frontend/**` provides concrete demand evidence through routes, screens, typed APIs, Mock adapters, tests, states, and copy. It may omit business rules or encode provisional assumptions, so inspect it read-only while extracting requirements and domain truth. Domainry Plane exposes backend capability contracts plus the transport-only `@domainry/runtime-client` the frontend consumes; Runtime machine contracts remain authoritative for data, authorization, navigation, and operation semantics. Frontend source stays read-only throughout the task; the recorded scope and evidence-based defect routing below turn frontend-owned defects into findings, never edits. See [runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md) for the client contract the frontend binds against.\\n\\nThe fixed stage order is: requirements → domain facts → calculation ownership/persistence/correction → capability discovery → model → apply → verify → done. Requirements and domain facts always precede Runtime capability selection; platform shape never supplies business truth.\\n\\n## Batch scale\\n\\nFirst record `Execution scope: requirements_only` or `implementation`. Use `requirements_only` only when the user requests analysis, requirements, or domain design and explicitly excludes development. It keeps the header, requirements, domain-truth design, and done sections; omit model, apply, Runtime, and verify sections with that reason in the header. Its `done` proves only the accepted requirements/domain artifact and must report no delivery or L1-L5 implementation claim. Any build, repair, integration, or end-to-end request is `implementation` and follows the normal full/light rules below.\\n\\nClassify every batch as `full` or `light` and record the classification with its justification in the TODO header. A light batch is an existing-project increment with no new Object, Action, role, permission, scope, or menu change; everything else is a full batch. In a light batch, instantiate the TODO with only the sections the batch touches: always keep the header, the execution rules, and the `verify` and `done` stages; under `model` and `apply`, copy only the touched subsections and record every omitted subsection in the header `Sections omitted` field with what the batch does not change and the prior accepted evidence reference. When the batch changes no Action, role, permission, scope, or menu, the entire authorization and navigation acceptance section is omitted this way. Inside a copied section, a checkbox whose subject the batch does not touch may still be closed as not applicable with the same scope evidence; a subject the batch touches is never omitted or closed as not applicable. If work discovered mid-batch breaks any light criterion, reclassify the batch as full in the TODO and complete the full-track items before proceeding. A full batch follows every stage without merging.\\n\\nThe caller-explicit `source-finalization-continuation` route is selected and initialized from the loaded entrypoint before any whole-project resource is read. It never loads this guide, the TODO template, or another whole-project reference; its compact Gate-owned envelope is its complete workflow. For every normal whole-project session, use the following initialization unchanged.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"session-todo-and-gate-receipts\\\"></a>\\n\\nAt the start of an Agent session:\\n\\n1. Resolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once.\\n2. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, the Agent session/thread ID, and the TODO path to the resolver's returned `cli`, `session_id`, and `todo`; retain its machine-readable `authoring_context` and compact `resume_packet` across context compaction instead of repeating broad capability/schema/reference discovery. Require that CLI to be the package's single `domainry-cli` binary and verify it with `version --json`.\\n3. Resolve the session ID from the resolver. Its primary sources are `DOMAINRY_AGENT_SESSION_ID` in Domainry, `CODEX_THREAD_ID` in Codex, or an equivalent explicit Host value; the temporary Domainry fallback is the unique active log session whose `cwd` equals the exact project root. Treat the ID as an opaque safe filename segment. Never generate a replacement UUID, select the globally newest log session, accept an ambiguous project match, guess, shorten, borrow, or reuse another session's ID.\\n4. If `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md` already exists, resume it because it belongs to the same session.\\n5. Otherwise create that exact file from `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md`.\\n6. Record the exact session ID, batch goal, original requirement, project mode, execution scope, scope, one `Frontend mutation scope`, and `requirements` as the initial current stage before implementation.\\n7. Add concrete project tasks beneath the template items. Do not keep a second execution plan.\\n8. Run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" sync` once. Execute the TODO strictly top-to-bottom per the template's execution rules; never check later work merely because it happened out of order. Close checkboxes through the evidence Gate's `run`, `attach`, or explicit `mark --status not_applicable` commands, not by writing success prose. Before leaving a stage, require `status --stage <stage> --require-complete`.\\n9. At completion, close every item and finding, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` once immediately before the final response. Repair every reported inconsistency and rerun the checker until it passes.\\n\\nOne Agent session corresponds to one TODO document and owns only `.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md`; never overwrite or update another session's document. A new session creates a new file even for related work and may reference prior session TODOs only as read-only handoff context.\\n\\nThe TODO is the only progress ledger. Do not keep a second execution plan. Append validation findings to this document, finish the reachable round, batch repairs, and revalidate until the finding inventory is empty. One artifact may support several items, but every Gate binds the artifact and exact fact proved. Changed evidence content or a bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime manifest, identity, or receipt-named artifact makes the Gate stale and requires current evidence. Keep evidence concise and link to large artifacts instead of pasting logs. The lightweight TODO checker only detects document consistency; it runs no build or Runtime work and is not a second completion authority, evidence database, or source-wide staleness system.\\n\\nKeep `assets/DEVELOPMENT_TODO.template.md` in the same dependency order as this guide; if the template order is wrong, correct and validate the template before using or releasing that Skill version — never work around a misordered template by executing later sections first.\\n\\n## Round discipline\\n\\nFor every validation round:\\n\\n1. Run every safely reachable check in the current stage.\\n2. Record the complete finding inventory in the TODO with `open` or `resolved` state and concrete evidence.\\n3. Group findings by shared root cause and module.\\n4. Repair the complete batch.\\n5. Re-run affected focused checks, then only the stage checks invalidated by the repair.\\n6. Repeat until no open finding remains.\\n\\nStop early only when continuation is impossible, risks data loss or security, corrupts protected state, or invalidates the remaining results. A recoverable failure is not a reason to stop the round.\\n\\n## Semantic execution contract and canary\\n\\n<a id=\\\"semantic-execution-and-canary\\\"></a>\\n\\nBefore model authoring, close one semantic execution row for every acceptance journey in the existing backend PRD; reference those rows from the session TODO instead of creating a second plan. Each row binds:\\n\\n- the requirement and actor to a project-provisioned Runtime identity and formal session/principal source;\\n- every Runtime-owned context fact or canonical identifier to the typed client, formal seed/admin path, or persisted Runtime response that supplies it;\\n- fresh setup identities/records and any explicit dependency on another journey;\\n- the command surface and the actor-visible read surface;\\n- a positive oracle naming the target identity and required non-empty facts;\\n- the exact denial, concealment, validation, or conflict status/error category plus unchanged-state oracle;\\n- the durable database effect, replay/restart requirement, and authoritative reconciliation source.\\n\\nRuntime-owned identities, hierarchy values, paths, claims, cursors, and fingerprints are opaque values. Carry the formal returned value unchanged across metadata, seed data, Handler inputs, persisted records, and acceptance. Do not synthesize them from display names or conventions, and do not trim, append, case-fold, or otherwise normalize them unless the published Runtime contract explicitly owns that transformation. A missing formal source is a blocking Foundation/Runtime gap, not permission to inject a request claim or guess a representation.\\n\\nFor each scope-sensitive row, prepare one target record and probe it through real sessions with otherwise equivalent requests: the matching actor observes that exact record and a non-empty expected result; the non-matching actor receives the contractually specified concealment/denial and produces no durable effect; when a broader application role is in scope, it observes the same record. A success status with an empty collection, null result, zero aggregate, or wrong target is a failure unless the confirmed requirement calls for that exact value. A negative probe passes only on its intended stable status/error category and unchanged state, never on an arbitrary `4xx`, `not found`, or setup failure.\\n\\nChoose one highest-risk row as the semantic canary, favoring a journey that crosses the most of identity/context resolution, data scope, project Action, multi-record persistence, actor-visible projection, and restart/replay. After the full model is applied, source is finalized, and focused tests pass, execute that row before the broad Runtime integration round. If it fails, mark every dependent journey blocked by that root cause, finish the reachable failure inventory, repair the owner once, and rerun the canary plus only invalidated checks. Do not spend a full acceptance round rediscovering the same upstream mismatch, but never use the canary to shrink the complete model or final denominator.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-and-frontend-boundaries\\\"></a>\\n\\n## Project and frontend boundaries\\n\\nThe canonical project-owned authoring surfaces are:\\n\\n```text\\nbackend/model/*.json  declarative backend metadata inventory\\nbackend/reports/*.sql  project-owned Report SQL referenced by model sql_file\\nbackend/               project-owned Go source and tests plus generated platform source\\n.domainry/development/<agent-session-id>.md  current session TODO\\n.domainry/             CLI-managed delivery, contracts, receipts, artifacts, Runtime, and evidence\\n```\\n\\nGit owns project-source development, so normal editors, patches, formatters, dependency tools, and Git worktrees may modify project-owned source. `.domainry/development/*.md` is the project-owned coordination exception under `.domainry/**`, with each file owned only by its matching Host session. Never hand-edit generated source; frozen Project Contracts; Runtime manifests; materialization, finalization, verification, or package receipts; audits, caches, journals, managed Runtime state, or other CLI-owned delivery state. Do not create symlinks, traversal paths, special files, placeholders, empty success handlers, or business source that never uses its generated capability set.\\n\\nRecord exactly one `Frontend mutation scope` in the TODO header:\\n\\n- `integration_authorized`: the user requested a build, fix-all repair, integration, or end-to-end acceptance without narrowing the task to backend-only, and repository evidence confirms project ownership;\\n- `backend_only`: the user explicitly limited work to backend;\\n- `externally_owned`: another tool or owner delivered the frontend;\\n- `unauthorized`: no current request authorizes frontend mutation.\\n\\nThe scope records authorization context only and never changes this Skill's edit boundary: `frontend/**` remains read-only in every scope throughout the task. During integration compare the requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real response before assigning ownership. Repair a backend or Runtime gap at its owner; never hide it behind a compatibility adapter, raw HTTP, fabricated fallback, altered Mock, or weakened test. Record a frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, minimal reproduction, and contract basis. If it blocks a backend journey, mark that journey blocked without relabeling the defect as a platform gap.\\n\\nEnd-to-end acceptance here means backend end-to-end through real managed-Runtime HTTP/SSE/worker/database behavior bound to the TODO and Gate source identity. Browser-layer UI verification is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n## New and existing projects\\n\\nFor a new project:\\n\\n- Before any project-mutating `domainry-cli` command, require the exact project root to be one initialized Git worktree. If the new root is not inside another worktree, run `git init --initial-branch=main <project-root>` once and verify `git -C <project-root> rev-parse --show-toplevel` equals the exact root. Never retry mutations before this check, initialize an existing project without authorization, or create a nested repository inside another worktree.\\n- Preserve the full requirement; do not silently reduce it to an MVP, demo, phase, or core subset.\\n- Establish users, roles, goals, lifecycles, exceptional paths, and the complete operation inventory before modeling.\\n- Apply industry business practices where they clarify the requested product without expanding its scope.\\n- Before first backend publication, model local/test-only non-human acceptance identities, their formal login/session path, required Workforce profiles and assignments, departments/jobs/positions, business menu definitions, role-menu assignments, and the minimum real records for every acceptance journey. Do not defer these prerequisites to verify or seed online credentials.\\n\\nFor an existing project:\\n\\n- Read current requirement documents, Git state, backend source, metadata, tests, and managed delivery state.\\n- Record current behavior and the affected dependency closure in the TODO.\\n- Plan only the requested increment and required regression coverage.\\n- Retain valid existing source; do not rewrite it to manufacture workflow evidence.\\n\\n## Initialization\\n\\n<a id=\\\"cli-identity-and-canonical-command-map\\\"></a>\\n\\nResolve `SKILL_ROOT` from the exact loaded `SKILL.md` path and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-context.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` once at task start. Bind `DOMAINRY_CLI`, session ID, TODO, compact `resume_packet`, one-time `authoring_context`, routed reference paths, and package identity to the result. `DOMAINRY_CLI` must equal the same package's single executable `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-cli\\\"` (`domainry-cli.exe` on Windows). Re-run the resolver only if the exact project root or Host session identity changes. Never resolve a CLI through `PATH`, substitute another Skill, or call Domainry Plane directly. Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json` before every other Builder command. These actions are setup rather than stages or TODO gates.\\n\\nUse only the applicable command families below, in TODO order. Add one pinned `--environment <name>` or `--service <url>` target wherever shown and keep it consistent. Use `--help` only to resolve listed-command flags; do not invent neighboring commands. The packaged CLI has no update, sync, doctor, audit, repair, rollback, migration, or generic contract-search surface.\\n\\n```text\\n# initialization and SDK bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" version --json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [target]\\n\\n# fixed capability discovery\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-category --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --key <fixed-category-key>\\n\\n# complete declarative model, inspection, and one-time legacy bootstrap\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model validate --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# the only release command; an existing delivery may require its emitted evolution review\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <business_workspace|consumer_portal|business_workspace,consumer_portal> [target] [--allow-destructive]\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <same-reviewed-surfaces> [target] --evolution-audit-sha256 <emitted-sha256> --evolution-reason <reviewed-reason>\\n\\n# approved source gaps\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" action source apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" [--evolution-audit-sha256 <sha256>] ... --input - <object.action>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector definition --json [target] <connector>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector context --projection coding --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" <connector.provider>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" connector scaffold apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" ... <connector.provider>\\n\\n# source closure in this exact order before developer tests\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n# verification, package, managed Runtime, and exact acceptance denominator\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project verify --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project package --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process start --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --bundle <current-project-relative-bundle> --address 127.0.0.1:<dedicated-port>\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process status --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance prepare --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance run --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --cases standard --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance stale-report --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project acceptance check --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --results backend/tests/evidence/backend-acceptance-results.json\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" runtime-process stop --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n```\\n\\nThe only current managed project-state root is the stable `.domainry/builder/**`. The first valid Builder project command uses `domainry-builder-state-root-migration-v1` to discover and atomically rename any single real legacy `.domainry/builder-*` root under the cross-version project mutation lock. The CLI refuses to merge or overwrite whenever more than one current/legacy root exists, rejects symlink or non-directory roots, and normalizes only persisted path-valued JSON prefixes while preserving protocol/history strings. Do not move, copy, merge, or recreate any managed root by hand.\\n\\n## requirements\\n\\n<a id=\\\"requirements-to-domain-facts\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Produce the backend dependency PRD\\n\\nFor a new project or a complete frontend Mock-to-Runtime replacement, create or update `docs/backend-requirements-prd.md` from `assets/BACKEND_REQUIREMENTS_PRD.template.md`. This is not a marketing PRD or a pixel inventory: it is the accepted demand contract for what Runtime and project metadata/source must provide. For a light existing-project increment, record an equivalent traceable PRD delta in the current session TODO or update the accepted backend PRD, and reference the unchanged baseline.\\n\\n**Reference mode for structured input.** A source is structured when its rules live in stable titled/numbered sections citable as `<doc> §N`. Test per requirement: if one section states the rule completely, cite it — do not restate it; rewrite only what must be assembled from scattered evidence (Mock adapters, tests, verbal decisions, multiple documents). A reference-mode PRD carries requirement IDs, classifications, owner/capability mapping, decisions, acceptance criteria, and the matrices, each row citing its source section; restating an already-structured source is prohibited waste. Both modes keep the Resolve-critical-ambiguity table and full traceability.\\n\\nRead frontend evidence in this order: route/page registry and user journeys; typed application API; Mock adapters/fixtures; state stores and hooks; form/list/detail/report components; permission/menu guards; tests and stories; visible copy. Also read the current explicit requirement and latest accepted product/design documents. Legacy APIs or prior backends are `legacy_reference` until the current requirement or frontend confirms them.\\n\\nThis inspection is read-only even when later integration is authorized to repair frontend source. A Mock fixture, adapter behavior, test expectation, or visible affordance proves demand or a current assumption; it does not establish a business rule without higher-precedence evidence.\\n\\nUse this precedence when evidence conflicts:\\n\\n1. explicit current user decision;\\n2. latest accepted requirement or design contract;\\n3. latest implemented frontend behavior;\\n4. typed API and Mock semantics;\\n5. frontend tests;\\n6. legacy/reference implementation.\\n\\nDo not erase conflicts using precedence alone. Record the conflict, affected requirement IDs, selected authority, and confirmation evidence. Classify every item as `confirmed`, `recommended`, `open`, `frontend_only`, or `legacy_reference`.\\n\\nExtract at least:\\n\\n- pages, routes, entry conditions, actors, journeys, and observable success;\\n- canonical object identities, fields, relations, list/detail/form/report shapes, filters, search, sort, and pagination;\\n- commands, outcomes, bulk operations, state transitions, approvals, cancellation/correction paths, and durable side effects;\\n- roles, permissions, record scope, sensitive fields, menu visibility, and allow/deny behavior;\\n- validation, stable errors, conflicts, optimistic concurrency, transaction boundaries, idempotency, and replay behavior;\\n- calculations with inputs, units, formula, policy/version date, rounding point/mode, examples, and refund/correction behavior;\\n- schedulers, automations, notifications, connectors, files, audit, outbox, and external effects;\\n- presentation-only behavior that remains `frontend_only` and must not create backend metadata.\\n\\nBuild a traceability matrix with one row per requirement: `frontend evidence → requirement ID → Runtime owner/capability → model resource or approved source gap → permission/data scope → acceptance journey`. Where the frontend exposes a typed API, count its backend-bound operations and account for every one in the matrix as implemented, platform-reused, frontend-only, superseded, or open. Every route must map to a journey or an explicit frontend-only disposition. Every write operation must name authorization, validation/error behavior, transaction boundary, idempotency/retry policy, durable result, and acceptance proof.\\n\\nMatrix and contract-table cells are phrases, not sentences. Once a fact is in a table (or cited by section), do not restate it in prose; prose is only for what no table column captures.\\n\\n### Resolve critical ambiguity\\n\\nDo not infer any unresolved object identity, financial or payroll formula, permission boundary, state transition, inventory effect, or external side effect. Keep the affected requirement and downstream modeling items blocked until the user or accepted requirement evidence confirms the rule. A visual button, fixture value, label, or legacy endpoint proves demand exists but does not prove the business rule.\\n\\nNon-critical open items may proceed only when the PRD records the assumption, decision owner, fallback, and excluded acceptance scope. Never mark them `confirmed` merely because implementation is possible.\\n\\n## domain truth design\\n\\n<a id=\\\"domain-truth-design\\\"></a>\\n\\nRead [domain-truth-and-settlement.md](domain-truth-and-settlement.md) completely. Freeze the domain facts before asking what Runtime can implement; close every calculation's ownership/persistence/correction decisions per that document. A capability is an implementation option after these facts exist; it is not evidence for choosing them.\\n\\nFinancial, payroll, points, inventory-cost, and settlement facts must remain reproducible after Settings change. If an event locks a result, model the write-side atomic Action and immutable snapshot/ledger before its Reports. Keep every affected model item blocked until this Gate closes.\\n\\n### Mandatory list Report and export Gate\\n\\nBefore discovering or selecting a Report capability, close every applicable contract below as a blocking design and acceptance Gate:\\n\\n1. **List Report pagination:** every list Report has bounded server pagination: platform standard or `page_size` plus opaque cursor, maximum page size 200, stable ordering with a unique cursor tie-breaker, explicit `truncated`, and `total_semantics` of `exact` or `at_least`. A transport page that materializes the complete result before slicing is not bounded execution.\\n2. **Same-Report threshold routing:** export prepare makes one bounded probe of the same Report under normalized parameters and current authorization/data Scope. The fixed threshold is 1000: an exhausted `0..1000` probe yields the exact total and one synchronous governed CSV; observing row 1001 immediately routes to the platform asynchronous paged export-job substrate, whose terminal result records the final exact total. Both branches use the identical Report/SQL/source hash/parameters/Scope; no count-only or export-only second query is allowed.\\n3. **Asynchronous reauthorization and terminal notification:** before asynchronous page one, the worker reconstructs the current persisted project user-role principal in the frozen workspace and compares canonical authorization Scope, failing closed on real drift. It never substitutes a manifest-only role or synthetic Surface. Only this branch publishes requester-only `completed`/`failed` Inbox notifications linked through export job, audit, and artifact.\\n4. **Atomic fingerprint replay:** the server canonicalizes Report key, normalized parameters, SQL/source hash, requester/workspace, authorization/data-Scope hash, format, and result version. Equivalent requests atomically reuse an accepted/running job or a completed unexpired artifact without a second file, download record, or terminal notification. `audit_id` stays out of the fingerprint; only failed/cancelled work or expired/corrupt artifacts may create one new execution through database compare-and-create.\\n\\nRequire `pagination_trace`, a signed cursor strategy, and `query_profile` evidence captured through `EXPLAIN QUERY PLAN` on the SQLite acceptance database; never provision a second engine only for profiling. Read [capabilities/reports.md](capabilities/reports.md) whenever a page/API returns Report rows or offers Report export. If the delivery cannot prove the contracts, classify the in-scope Report list/export as `acceptance_failed` with a Runtime Report owner gap. Never substitute browser-memory pagination, repeated full-result execution per chunk, generic batch download, duplicate artifacts, a second query, invented infrastructure, or excluded scope.\\n\\nBefore capability selection or model authoring, read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md) completely and derive G01-G32 applicability from the current requirements/model demand pool. Re-read it during verify and close every applicable static plus real-Runtime proof; do not wait until terminal acceptance to discover a missing transaction, index, timeout, provider lifecycle, SSE resume, data-scope isolation or recovery contract.\\n\\n## model\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-and-capability-mapping\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Derive and reconcile resources\\n\\nInspect the delivered frontend's typed API layer and Mock adapters read-only, together with the requirement documents. Extract every displayed/input/filtered/sorted/related field, query, report, operation, role, transition, transaction, permission, scope, approval, schedule, automation, notification, external effect, error/conflict contract, Identity menu entry, and role-menu assignment the frontend Mock contract or requirement expresses. Keep each demand's source and classification; do not turn a Mock assumption into model truth.\\n\\n- Object identity is the business entity.\\n- Relation identity is source + field + target + cardinality.\\n- Action identity is semantics + target + transition + transaction boundary.\\n- Workflow and state-machine identity is the business lifecycle.\\n- Assign one owner module to each canonical resource.\\n- Merge compatible demands and union compatible field uses.\\n- Report incompatible field, relation, transition, transaction, or role demands together.\\n- Do not duplicate resources, use last-write-wins, or clone an Action because roles differ.\\n- Configure menu definitions through the foundation Identity menu management boundary and assign them to roles there.\\n\\n### Map accepted facts to capabilities\\n\\nOnly after domain truth closes, prefer Runtime CRUD, standard record operations, state machine, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, record views, Reports, and published Connector operations. Create project-owned Handler or Connector source only for a concrete capability gap that metadata cannot express. Platform capability decides this mapping, never the business fact.\\n\\nBefore recording any platform owner gap, ensure the delivery-owned Runtime Client SDK and disclosure contract are materialized: when absent or invalid, run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project materialize runtime-client --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"` with the batch's pinned Domainry Plane target. A fetch, signature, cache, receipt, or package failure is a delivery blocker; never fake, copy, alias, or bypass the SDK ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)).\\n\\nThen discover platform capability through the fixed category index: `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" contract capability-categories` once, then each selected `contract capability-category` batch once; register both levels plus the operation-to-endpoint mapping as TODO evidence and reconcile route, reuse classification, Surface projection, owner permission, data scope, and machine schema separately ([runtime-client.md](runtime-client.md)). Treat `operations_only` as an existing isolated capability, not backend absence. Agent task operations and diagnostics are operations-only even though authorized Business routes exist under `/agent-dialog/**`; never move or call the operations routes from project source. Reject unknown keys and never use free-text `contract search` for Admin capability discovery.\\n\\n## apply\\n\\n<a id=\\\"model-apply-is-the-only-release\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Author the model files and release\\n\\nClose every module's Object, Relation, Action, Role, authorization, data scope, Workflow, state machine, Report, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, seed, and acceptance inventory before authoring the backend model files. Declare that complete inventory as items in `backend/model/*.json`, grouped by owning module — typically one file per module; the exact file contract lives in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md).\\n\\nBefore the first `model validate`, close the cross-resource preflight and strict nested-shape card in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md): exact Identity menu/role-menu fields, `min`/`max` validation keys, array-valued report metrics, the exact Report emission/`object_sql_v1` card (`value.key`, alias-only join cardinalities, and bounded SQL), closed export-control fields, and bidirectional behavior/resource ownership. Partial-resource or intermediate-file validation is a guaranteed-invalid checkpoint and is prohibited. Repair the complete diagnostic batch before revalidating. After validation first reaches `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, freeze the model draft while recording evidence; a requirement-backed change starts a new atomic draft and requires `model validate` plus `model plan` again.\\n\\nThe model files are the single backend authoring surface and the source of truth. Inspect the pending change set with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model plan --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`; bootstrap an older project once with `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model export --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"`. Do not edit `domain/**` or `.domainry/**` Ledger state by hand ([backend-model.md](backend-model.md)).\\n\\nWhen the module inventories are complete, release the Blueprint once:\\n\\n```text\\n\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" model apply --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --go-module-path <domain-qualified-module> --product-surfaces <reviewed-surfaces> [one pinned explicit --environment or --service target]\\n```\\n\\n`model apply` is the only normal-development release command. The complete apply semantics — stage chaining, checklist gating, destructive refusal and `--allow-destructive`, rename = delete + create, receipt path, `--sync-only`, and the prohibition on direct Domainry Plane calls or split publish/materialize maintenance commands — are defined in [backend-model.md](backend-model.md). Always pass reviewed `--product-surfaces`; it is the backend-to-frontend integration boundary used by materialization, not authorization to generate or redesign page source. After a stage failure, repair the model files or reported input and rerun the same command. An existing delivery that returns `evolution_review_required` may rerun only with the emitted audit SHA-256 and reviewed reason.\\n\\nBackend materialization inside `model apply` installs backend templates, generated/source inputs, contracts, Runtime manifest, and Go dependencies. It must never create, modify, or delete `frontend/**`, including frontend paths contained in Project Source Seed.\\n\\nBackend materialization is not the local-composition compile closure. The project template may already call `composition.RuntimeOptions`, while `generated/composition/extensions.gen.go`, the Action registry, and the Connector registry remain owned by source finalization. Complete that closure only with `project source finalize`.\\n\\n### Implement and integrate\\n\\n<a id=\\\"source-prepare-implementation-packet\\\"></a>\\n\\n- Run `\\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source prepare --json --project <project>` immediately after materialization. Treat the bounded envelope's Action key/path/state list as the Handler implementation inventory and retain the exact `.implementation_packet.path`, payload/file hashes, and delivery/materialization identity. The CLI atomically persists the complete content-addressed packet under managed `.domainry/builder/**`; never redirect stdout to create it and never use or invent `latest.json`. First inspect the envelope's small Action index, then use local `jq` for exactly one packet `actions[]` entry selected by `action_key` and only the referenced `documents[]` content selected by SHA. Never `cat` or print the complete packet, and do not issue one `action context` command per Action in the same installed batch. The command creates only missing canonical project-owned Handler/test skeletons, never replaces an existing file, and writes in bounded atomic batches. Use focused `action context` only for an isolated repair or an audit-bound pending evolution absent from the installed packet.\\n- Compileability of a prepared skeleton proves only contract shape. Every prepared `*.not_implemented` result remains fail closed and must be replaced by accepted business behavior before source finalization can pass.\\n- Obtain bounded coding context for approved source gaps.\\n- Implement Handler, domain, Connector, and tests by module. Replace each generated placeholder test with typed non-nil Execution/capability stubs plus current output/effect assertions; retain separate hand-written nil/authorization robustness tests when they remain valuable.\\n- Use generated typed capabilities and real data access.\\n- Reject placeholders, empty success returns, and handlers that never use their capability set.\\n- Complete authorization, transaction, idempotency, audit, outbox, and errors.\\n- Execute `project check`, `project source finalize`, and each focused Go test through the matching development Gate `run` on the command's first real execution, using the exact forms in Project check to finalize to tests below. Re-run only gates invalidated by a later Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change.\\n- On a failed checklist, source finalization, or project verification, inspect the archived receipt and repair every `failed` row from the same round; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass ([verification.md](verification.md)). Do not rerun after repairing only the first console message.\\n- Run module-focused compile/tests, then the cross-module suite. A focused-test Gate accepts only a real test argv (for Go, `run --cwd backend -- go test ...`); a finalization receipt, unrelated argv, or free note cannot prove it.\\n- Run the selected semantic canary against the managed Runtime and accepted database. Require formal actor/context resolution, exact target identity, a non-empty positive result, intended scope/error contrast, durable effect/read-back, and unchanged denied/conflict state before broad integration.\\n- Validate real API binding, authorization, persistence, idempotency, and reconciliation against the managed Runtime.\\n- Reconcile every published operation against the explicit requirement, typed frontend API, published Runtime contract, Mock/tests, and real Runtime response. Assign each mismatch to its actual backend, Runtime, or frontend owner before repair. A missing/wrong Runtime route, schema, authorization, scope, persistence, or error contract is repaired at that owner; never mask it with a frontend compatibility layer, raw HTTP, fake fallback, or weakened test.\\n- Apply the recorded `Frontend mutation scope` from Project and frontend boundaries as an authorization record only: under every scope frontend source stays read-only — record each frontend- or Mock-owned defect as a finding with owner `frontend`, a minimal reproduction, and its contract basis, and block the affected journey when the defect truly blocks backend acceptance rather than relabeling it as an owner gap.\\n- Rerun the real Runtime journey after backend repairs ([verification.md](verification.md)), binding all evidence to the same TODO Gate source hash covering backend and domain source. Browser-layer UI regression is outside this Skill's acceptance scope.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"project-check-to-finalize-to-tests\\\"></a>\\n\\n### Project check to finalize to tests\\n\\nObtain the matching Gate IDs from one `list` result. Run every check, finalize, and focused-test command through `run` on its first real execution so the returned bounded envelope's `evidence_path` is already bound to that exact Gate. Never execute a product command directly first, rerun it merely to obtain a receipt, search historical receipts, or attach another Gate's receipt with an explanatory note.\\n\\n```sh\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$INVALID_INVENTORY_GATE\\\" --bind source --expect-exit 1 \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state invalid \\\\\\n  --expect-json-diagnostic-code project.action_test_scaffold_stale -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$VALID_CHECK_GATE\\\" --bind source \\\\\\n  --expect-json-contract domainry-builder-v6-project-check-v1 --expect-json-state valid -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project check --json --scope actions --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FINALIZE_GATE\\\" --bind source -- \\\\\\n  \\\"$DOMAINRY_CLI\\\" project source finalize --json --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\"\\n\\n\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-gates.py\\\" --project \\\"$PROJECT_ROOT\\\" --session-id \\\"$SESSION_ID\\\" run \\\\\\n  --gate-id \\\"$FOCUSED_TEST_GATE\\\" --bind source --cwd backend -- \\\\\\n  go test ./actions/...\\n```\\n\\nThe expected-nonzero form is permitted only for a TODO label containing both `project check` and `diagnostic inventory`, and only when the first check is genuinely expected to expose a repair inventory. `run` returns 0 when its declared expectation is met while preserving the product exit in `command_exit_code`. The complete bounded JSON contract, state, scope, and diagnostic-code inventory must match; an inconsistent, unexpected, or beyond-bound result fails the Gate but still returns the new receipt path for diagnosis. The later valid check separately requires exit 0, `state:\\\"valid\\\"`, actions scope, and an empty diagnostic inventory.\\n\\nEach receipt records exact argv/cwd/exit, start/completion/duration, bounded stdout/stderr byte counts and SHA-256 rather than streams, selected bindings, current source/Blueprint/Runtime identities, and bounded project-check or current finalization proof. Secret-bearing argv and credential URIs are rejected before execution; environment variables are never serialized. A receipt belongs to its original Gate and cannot be rebound, so finalization can never prove a test Gate. Changed bound source, model/Blueprint, Runtime, evidence content, or a receipt-named artifact makes it stale.\\n\\nBefore every source finalize and developer Go test, `project check --scope actions` must pass. Its stale-scaffold scan rejects a delivery-bound implemented Handler whose canonical generated test still invokes nil Execution or asserts that Action's `*.not_implemented` result, and returns the complete bounded stable `project.action_test_scaffold_stale` inventory without running tests or emitting source. For a dependency-resolved materialized Go backend with Actions it also performs the offline, readonly production compile closure for `./actions/...`; it disables workspace use, toolchain auto-download, VCS, and module/network lookup. Finalize repeats the stale-scaffold preflight, activates local composition, closes dependencies, and enforces applicable compilation before publishing a success receipt. Failure publishes no new successful finalization receipt and rolls back activated changes where required. Re-finalize after any Action, Connector, domain, or composition-input change. Only a current complete finalization receipt permits format, lint, vet, focused tests, or full tests. Repair every `failed` receipt row as one batch; `skipped_dependency` is not a pass.\\n\\n### Accept authorization and navigation against real data\\n\\nRun this required acceptance after backend implementation and real Runtime/database data acceptance, before the apply stage can close:\\n\\n1. Read the complete Action inventory from the current published Runtime metadata and record the total.\\n2. Write Action authorization matrix rows with delta scope. For a full batch (a new project or an increment that changes authorization broadly), write one row per published Action and require the matrix row count to equal the published total. For an existing-project increment, write one row per Action added or changed in this batch and one summary row per unchanged Action group that references the prior accepted matrix evidence (previous session TODO path and date); the batch rows plus referenced rows must still cover the published total. Each authored row records Action identity, target/Object, execution kind, published permission or exact `allowed_roles`, required data scope, one allowed probe, one denied probe, current Runtime or persisted evidence, and result. An Action without a permission owner, allowed role, required scope, or denied proof is a blocking mismatch.\\n3. Inspect the actual Runtime database through the supported project database/inspection path after seed and business-data acceptance. Record the query or durable evidence identifiers for persisted menu definitions, role-menu assignments, roles, and other permission state used by the matrix. Do not infer persisted state from Blueprint or source alone.\\n4. For every application role affected by this batch, the Agent creates or reuses a real Runtime session through a project-provisioned non-human acceptance identity and calls `GET /identity/effective-menus?surface=business_workspace`. For an existing-project increment, roles with no menu, permission, or route change in this batch may reference the prior accepted effective-menu evidence instead of a fresh session. Compare returned menu identities and routes with the persisted menu definitions and role-menu assignments. Record missing, unexpected, disabled, or cross-role entries. Never ask the user to log in, switch roles, repeat login, or provide personal credentials. Use the formal project seed/admin path to provision acceptance identities when authorized; otherwise record the missing safe identity/session path as an environment or Foundation blocker.\\n5. Record totals and the complete Action authorization and role-effective-menu matrices in the current TODO. Finish the whole round, batch-repair every mismatch, repeat the database/API comparisons, and proceed only when mismatch totals are zero.\\n\\nMetadata checklist success or isolated allow tests cannot replace this acceptance.\\n\\n### Review and converge\\n\\nRun a complete backend/integration round covering APIs, business journeys, the completed authorization/navigation matrices, allowed/denied authorization, data-scope isolation, sensitive fields, durable effects, validation, conflicts, idempotency, Workflow, Scheduler, Automation, Connector, audit, and outbox.\\n\\nFinish the round before repair, then batch-repair by owner group and re-run the invalidated backend checks until no open finding remains (Round discipline).\\n\\n## verify\\n\\n<a id=\\\"runtime-and-acceptance\\\"></a>\\n\\nAfter the backend converges, read [evidence-levels.md](evidence-levels.md) and [verification.md](verification.md) completely and re-read [production-technical-gates.md](production-technical-gates.md). `project verify` and packaging prove `delivery_verified` structural/release closure, not `scenario_verified` real-data behavior. Run the complete verify check-and-evidence inventory specified in verification — source boundary, backend/frontend suites, checklist/live validation, verify + package, managed Runtime, acceptance denominator prepare/harness/check, authorization/data-scope/idempotency journeys, matrix consistency, long/dense cases, and cleanup. Guide-specific ordering rules:\\n\\n- validate that the source-finalization receipt is still current; if source changes during repair, return to finalization before compiling or testing;\\n- confirm the persisted Action/permission and role/effective-menu matrices still match the restarted Runtime and accepted database.\\n\\nAfter the matching managed Runtime is healthy, run `project acceptance prepare`, then execute the platform runner **first** with `project acceptance run --cases standard` and inspect its exact-set v2 result rows. Build business harness assertions on `assets/acceptance-harness-core.py` only for `state:\\\"not_passed\\\"` rows with `disposition:\\\"needs_business_harness\\\"`. Repair `blocked_dependency`, `blocked_platform`, `unsupported_runner`, `setup_failed`, `evidence_rejected`, or `runtime_failed` at the named owner before rerunning. Then run `project acceptance check`. Every assertion obtains its session through `harness.session_for_case(case)`, uses `ScenarioGraph` for isolated setup/dependencies, and applies `ReasonOracle` for the declared status/error/state/audit/positive-control reason; never select a user from a role name, create profile headers, or read an internal identity table. The denominator is CLI-owned and never hand-edited. After a model evolution, run `project acceptance stale-report` and re-record only reported `stale`/`missing` cases, never the full set. Prose, arbitrary hashed text, or a coverage percentage cannot replace the harness.\\n\\nRecord the whole verify round before repair. If any finding exists, return it to its model or implementation owner, repair the batch, and run verify again (Round discipline).\\n\\n## done\\n\\n<a id=\\\"done-terminal-policy\\\"></a>\\n\\nRecord exactly one terminal state from [verification.md](verification.md): `done`, `acceptance_failed`, or `verification_blocked`, applying its failure classification and terminal policy. `done` requires every in-scope TODO item closed with concise current evidence, an empty finding inventory, a passing final verify round, every in-scope requirement mapped to implemented source and current validation evidence, and no requirement or limitation hidden, downgraded, or deferred. Product prerequisites such as identities, data, permissions, Runtime health, or persistence are implementation/Foundation defects, not environmental blockers. Do not claim `verified`, `accepted`, `working`, `scenario_verified`, or `done` above the highest evidence level reached; no structural pass upgrades delivery evidence into real-data behavior.\\n\\nFor any terminal state, require the applicable `verify` and `done` Gate status, close every finding according to the terminal policy, set `Current stage: done`, and run `\\\"$SKILL_ROOT/bin/domainry-development-todo-check.py\\\" --todo \\\"$TODO_PATH\\\"` immediately before the final response. Repair inconsistency and rerun until it passes. This linter pass is not test, Runtime, delivery, or scenario evidence.\\n\\nProvide a concise completion summary with implemented scope, test results, Runtime results, and explicitly non-blocking limitations.\\n\\n<a id=\\\"system-seed-login\\\"></a>\\n\\n## System seed login\\n\\nRuntime owns every initial credential. Never write credential rows, embed a password in model/source/TODO/evidence, or replace formal provisioning and password-change flows. A missing system/project seed credential is initialized from Runtime's deployment-owned `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD`; production startup rejects the built-in development value and the credential is always marked `must_change_password:true`. A user created through the formal Identity API receives a cryptographically random per-user password exactly once in a `Cache-Control: no-store` response and must change it. Startup fills only a missing credential; it never overwrites an existing password hash, clears a lock, or undoes a changed password.\\n\\nThe canonical system identity is user ID `admin`, login `admin@example.com`, Admin Console `/admin`, Surface `admin_console`. In a managed development Runtime started by this workflow, a deployment without `AUTH_DEFAULT_PASSWORD` seeds every missing credential — `admin` and project-declared acceptance identities — with development default `Domainry@2026`, marked `must_change_password:true`; production rejects this value. Establish a managed-development acceptance session by logging in with that default, completing forced change through `POST /auth/change-password` with `Idempotency-Key`, and keeping the new credential only in authorized secret handoff. Never print credentials into evidence or guess passwords: five failed logins produce `auth.account_locked`, and expiry does not lift the lock; an admin session must call `POST /identity/users/{userID}/unlock`. Formal Identity API users instead use their one-time no-store password. Business Workspace and Consumer Portal actors come from project-declared non-human acceptance identities. Authentication mode, session refresh, and authorization are independent of Surface; protected operations still enforce functional permission, ownership, Action policy, assurance, and data scope.\\n\",\"exit_code\":0,\"status\":\"completed\"}}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
